const GUIDES = {
  moon: {
    surface: "The Guidebook",
    keywords: ["Illusion", "Intuition", "Dreams", "The subconscious", "Fear", "The hidden"],
    meaning: { label: "What the card means", paras: ["The Moon is the night card of the deck. It stands for illusion, dreams, instinct, and everything a situation keeps beneath its surface. Where the Sun shows things exactly as they are, The Moon shows them as they feel, which is sometimes more honest and sometimes much less.", "Drawing it means some part of life is currently moonlit. The full picture has not come out yet, facts are missing, and imagination is busy filling the gaps. Fear tends to draw those gaps larger than they really are. The card carries an old reassurance: the road in the picture continues through the dark and comes out the other side. Confusion, here, is a phase, and phases pass."] },
    reading: { label: "As a reading", paras: ["Drawn tonight, The Moon points at the place in your life where certainty is thinnest. A decision that refuses to settle. A story that does not quite add up. A mood with no visible cause. It counsels patience over force: let the situation finish revealing itself before you act on it.", "It also asks you to take your intuition seriously. Under this card, a feeling is information arriving early. If something has seemed off lately, the card sides with you. And if fear has been doing the narrating, make it prove its claims. Moonlight exaggerates, and most of its monsters turn out to be rocks."] },
    closing: { label: "Before you return", para: "One card holds several truths, and the lenses are how this deck splits them apart. For The Moon they read: something that will not hold still, a pull you feel but cannot see, a deception you may already suspect, an instinct that has been right all along. Only you know which of those is tonight's truth.", line: "Turn toward the one that knows you." },
  },
  death: {
    surface: "The Guidebook",
    keywords: ["Endings", "Transformation", "Letting go", "Transition", "Rebirth", "Inevitable change"],
    meaning: { label: "What the card means", paras: ["Death is the deck's card of endings. It stands for transformation, transition, and the shedding of whatever has run its course. The name is the card's oldest bluff: it almost never points at literal loss. What it marks is the moment a chapter closes, and a closed chapter, here, is simply how the next one begins.", "Drawing it means something in your life has reached its natural end, whether or not that has been said out loud yet. The card is honest about the cost: endings take something with them, even the welcome ones. But its reassurance is built into its own logic. Whatever this card touches was already finished, and the space an ending clears does not stay empty for long."] },
    reading: { label: "As a reading", paras: ["In a reading, Death singles out whatever has already ended and is waiting to be admitted. A commitment kept past its purpose. A version of yourself you have outgrown. A situation everyone involved can feel winding down. Its counsel is to let the ending finish, because most of this card's weight sits in the holding on, and almost none of it in the letting go.", "Death's advice is to cooperate with the change rather than negotiate with it. If you are grieving something before it is officially over, trust that; part of you has already stepped through. Grief and relief often arrive together here, and both are allowed. Treat the moment the way a garden treats autumn: the cutting back looks harsh, and it is the only reason spring has anywhere to arrive."] },
    closing: { label: "Before you return", para: "One card holds several truths, and the lenses are how this deck splits them apart. For Death they read: an ending that leaves the ground richer, something old returning in a new form, a change so complete you hardly recognize the before, a way of doing things you ended yourself. Only you know which of those is tonight's truth.", line: "Turn toward the one that knows you." },
  },
  tower: {
    surface: "The Guidebook",
    keywords: ["Upheaval", "Revelation", "Breakdown of old structures", "Crisis", "Liberation"],
    meaning: { label: "What the card means", paras: ["The Tower is the card of sudden collapse. It stands for upheaval, shock, and the moment a structure gives way because it was built on something false. Where Death closes a chapter that had finished on its own, The Tower brings things down mid-sentence, without notice and without a vote.", "Drawing it means something you were standing on has cracked, or is about to: a certainty, a role, an arrangement you assumed was load-bearing. The card does not soften this; the fall is real, and it rearranges things. Its consolation is strict but genuine. Only the false part collapses. Whatever survives the shake was true all along, and now you know exactly which was which."] },
    reading: { label: "As a reading", paras: ["The Tower finds whatever has been standing on wishful thinking. A belief that just took a direct hit. A plan that came apart in a single afternoon. News that changed the shape of everything around it. Its counsel is to let the dust settle before you rebuild, because the urge to restack the same stones is strongest in the first hour.", "The card also carries an odd mercy: relief. When a false thing falls, the work of holding it up ends with it, and most people discover how tired they were only after it is gone. Do not rush to explain the collapse away. Stand in the open air it left, look hard at the foundations, and build the next thing on the part that held."] },
    closing: { label: "Before you return", para: "One card holds several truths, and the lenses are how this deck splits them apart. For The Tower they read: a demolition you would gladly start yourself, a blow that failed to finish you, a realization there is no walking back from, an order that deserved the defying. Only you know which of those is tonight's truth.", line: "Turn toward the one that knows you." },
  },
  fool: {
    surface: "The Guidebook",
    keywords: ["New beginnings", "Spontaneity", "Faith", "Leaping into the unknown", "Potential", "Innocence"],
    meaning: { label: "What the card means", paras: ["The Fool is the deck's beginning, numbered zero because the story has not started yet. It stands for new beginnings, spontaneity, and untested potential. Every other card knows where it is going. The Fool is the moment before that, the first step taken purely on faith.", "Drawing it means something in your life is at its very start: an idea, an urge, a door that only just opened. The card is cheerfully honest about the odds. A beginning this fresh cannot show you its ending, and the invitation stands anyway. Innocence here is an asset. The Fool moves precisely because it does not yet know every reason to stay put."] },
    reading: { label: "As a reading", paras: ["The Fool arrives beside the untried thing you keep circling. An opportunity that appeared out of nowhere. A reinvention you have been quietly rehearsing. An impulse that refuses to be talked down. Its counsel is to weigh the risk once, honestly, and then move, because a beginning studied for too long quietly becomes a decision not to.", "The Fool's advice is to stop waiting until you feel qualified. Readiness, under this card, is something the road hands you a little at a time, never something you can pack in advance. Treat the uncertainty as scenery rather than threat. In every printing of this card the Fool is mid-step at the cliff's edge, and the picture never once shows a fall."] },
    closing: { label: "Before you return", para: "One card holds several truths, and the lenses are how this deck splits them apart. For The Fool they read: a jump taken before the doubts could vote, a turn away from the marked road, a thing done purely for the joy of it, a gamble the sensible crowd would never place. Only you know which of those is tonight's truth.", line: "Turn toward the one that knows you." },
  },
  hermit: {
    surface: "The Guidebook",
    keywords: ["Solitude", "Introspection", "Soul-searching", "Guidance", "Inner wisdom"],
    meaning: { label: "What the card means", paras: ["The Hermit is the quietest card in the deck. It stands for solitude, introspection, and the kind of understanding that only arrives when the noise stops. The figure carries a lantern for a reason: this is a searching card, and the search is inward, conducted alone and entirely on purpose.", "Drawing The Hermit means some question in your life has stopped responding to conversation. More opinions will not settle it, and company, for the moment, is a kind of static. The card gives you permission to step back and hear yourself think. This is a retreat with a return built in: the Hermit withdraws to find something, and comes back once it is found."] },
    reading: { label: "As a reading", paras: ["The Hermit stands wherever you have run low on your own counsel. A decision crowded with other people's voices. A tiredness that company has stopped fixing. A question you have been asking everyone but yourself. Its counsel is to take the question somewhere quiet and sit with it longer than feels productive, because this card's answers are shy.", "There is a difference, this card insists, between loneliness and solitude. Loneliness is an emptiness that happens to you; the Hermit's solitude is chosen, furnished, and temporary, closer to an appointment than an exile. Keep it that way. Decline what needs declining without apology, and when you find what you went looking for, carry it back to the people who missed you. The lantern is lit for the walk home too."] },
    closing: { label: "Before you return", para: "One card holds several truths, and the lenses are how this deck splits them apart. For The Hermit they read: a truth that waits until nobody else is around, something better for taking its time, a find far from where the crowd gathers, a satisfaction that asked something of you first. Only you know which of those is tonight's truth.", line: "Turn toward the one that knows you." },
  },
  magician: {
    surface: "The Guidebook",
    keywords: ["Skill", "Willpower", "Manifestation", "Resourcefulness", "Inspired action", "Having all the tools"],
    meaning: { label: "What the card means", paras: ["The Magician is the card of making things happen. It stands for skill, will, and the discovery that the tools on the table are already enough. Where The Fool steps out with nothing but faith, The Magician arrives equipped: this card is about capability, and about aiming it at one thing on purpose.", "Drawing it means the gap between wanting and doing is currently thin. Conditions have lined up, the resources you already own are ready for use, and intention, for once, turns directly into results. The card puts its emphasis on deliberateness. What gets built now gets built because you chose it, shaped it, and saw it through, and that authorship is the point."] },
    reading: { label: "As a reading", paras: ["In practice, The Magician lands on the project that has been waiting for you to believe in it. A skill finally meeting its moment. An idea that needs assembling rather than more research. A conversation you are better prepared for than you think. Its counsel is to begin while everything is within reach, because this card describes a window, and windows close.", "The card's advice concerns focus. Capability spread across five projects reads as busyness; brought to bear on one, it reads as power. Pick the single thing that matters most and give it everything on the table. A stage illusion comes down to one practiced hand doing one thing supremely well while the whole room watches. So does this card."] },
  },
  high_priestess: {
    surface: "The Guidebook",
    keywords: ["Intuition", "Hidden knowledge", "Mystery", "Duality", "Patience", "The subconscious"],
    meaning: { label: "What the card means", paras: ["The High Priestess is the deck's keeper of what is known but not yet said. She stands for intuition, mystery, and the knowledge that sits beneath the surface of a situation. Where The Magician acts on the world, The High Priestess reads it. Hers is the quiet register of things, the felt, the sensed, and the not yet provable.", "Drawing her means a situation is running on two levels, and the official version is the thinner one. Something underneath carries the real information: an undercurrent in a room, a pattern you have registered without meaning to, a knowing you cannot yet defend. The card treats that lower level as entirely trustworthy. It simply has not surfaced yet, and it will."] },
    reading: { label: "As a reading", paras: ["She tends to appear where the facts have stopped adding new information. A decision that logic alone cannot close. A person whose words and signals disagree. An answer you already have but keep re-asking. Her counsel is stillness: stop gathering, stop polling the room, and let what you already know finish forming.", "Her advice is to give your inner sense the same respect you give evidence. You are not required to explain a hunch to anyone, including yourself, before honoring it. Make space where subtle things can register; noise is the only real enemy this card has. Think of deep water: it never advertises its depth, and it never has to."] },
  },
  empress: {
    surface: "The Guidebook",
    keywords: ["Nurturing", "Abundance", "Fertility", "Creativity", "Sensuality", "Comfort", "The natural world"],
    meaning: { label: "What the card means", paras: ["The Empress is the card of abundance. She stands for nurture, creativity, comfort, and the fertile side of life: everything that grows, feeds, ripens, or blooms. Hers is the most physical card in the deck. Where other cards live in the head or the future, The Empress lives in the senses, and she is doing fine there.", "Drawing her means some part of life is in a growing season. Work, a relationship, an idea, a home: something is responding to care and putting out shoots. The card asks you to keep tending rather than testing, because growth answers to attention. It also extends an invitation to enjoy the thing while it grows; pleasure, under this card, is evidence that the work is going well."] },
    reading: { label: "As a reading", paras: ["The Empress usually points at whatever is asking to be fed. A project in its delicate early growth. A relationship that flourishes when attended and wilts when scheduled around. A creative appetite that has been on rations lately. Her counsel is generosity toward all of it: give time, give material, give comfort, and let the results take the timeline they need.", "Her last word is for you rather than for the things you tend, since care flows outward from whoever holds the watering can. Let rest be genuinely restful, and let good things be enjoyed without an itemized justification. Sit in the garden you have been building instead of only weeding it. Nothing grows better for being watched anxiously, and everything grows better for being loved."] },
  },
  emperor: {
    surface: "The Guidebook",
    keywords: ["Authority", "Structure", "Stability", "Control", "Order", "Rules", "The Establishment", "Father figure", "Worldly power", "Rigidity"],
    meaning: { label: "What the card means", paras: ["The Emperor is the card of structure. He stands for authority, order, stability, and the discipline that turns ambition into something that lasts. He and The Empress rule adjoining territories: hers grows things, his builds them. This is the card of foundations poured, borders drawn, and systems that keep working on the days nobody feels inspired.", "Drawing him means some part of life is calling for a framework. Enthusiasm has done what enthusiasm does, and now the thing needs scaffolding: routines, boundaries, a clear decision about who holds what. The card sides with the builder. In the Emperor's view, the surest route to ease is a structure sound enough that you can stop thinking about it."] },
    reading: { label: "As a reading", paras: ["He shows up where things have been running on goodwill and improvisation. A commitment that needs its terms said out loud. A workload that needs a system instead of another heroic week. A boundary that keeps moving because it was never fixed anywhere. His counsel is to decide the rules once, clearly, and then let the rules do the enforcing.", "His advice is to take the authority you have been treating as optional, and to hold it calmly rather than apologetically. Steadiness is the whole performance; an order that must shout is already losing. Build the walls straight and the gates generous, and remember what walls are for. A kingdom is measured by what manages to flourish inside it."] },
  },
  hierophant: {
    surface: "The Guidebook",
    keywords: ["Tradition", "Institutions", "Belief systems", "Conformity", "Education", "Inherited wisdom"],
    meaning: { label: "What the card means", paras: ["The Hierophant is the deck's teacher. He stands for tradition, institutions, and knowledge that arrives with a lineage attached, tested by generations of hands before yours. Where The Emperor builds structures, The Hierophant maintains the ones that already work, and his classroom has been open a very long time.", "Drawing him means the situation you are in has been visited before, many times, and the accumulated notes are worth reading. Somewhere there is an established way through: a method, a mentor, a practice, a body of experience built for exactly this. The card counsels a practical kind of humility. Original thinking is expensive, and this may be a moment to spend inheritance instead."] },
    reading: { label: "As a reading", paras: ["Look for him wherever reinvention has quietly become the obstacle. A skill that wants formal grounding rather than another improvised attempt. A decision where the conventional answer happens to be the right one. A community whose accumulated sense you have been too proud to consult. His counsel is to ask the people who have done it, and then actually do it the way they say.", "The distinction he insists on is between rules that confine and rules that condense. The best traditions are compressed experience, centuries of trial and error folded into a form you can learn in an afternoon. Follow the form faithfully before you improvise on it. Mastery has always run in that order, and the ones who eventually depart from tradition beautifully are the ones who first learned exactly what it was holding up."] },
  },
  lovers: {
    surface: "The Guidebook",
    keywords: ["Union", "Harmony", "Attraction", "Relationships", "Choice", "Duality"],
    meaning: { label: "What the card means", paras: ["The Lovers is the card of union, and just as much the card of choice. It stands for attraction, partnership, and the moment two separate things decide to become one thing, along with everything that deciding costs. The romance in the name is real but never the whole story. This card is about what you commit to, and what you release in order to commit.", "Drawing it means a genuine choice is on the table, the kind made with values rather than spreadsheets. Two paths, two loyalties, or two versions of a life are asking you to stop keeping both alive. The card refuses to treat that as a burden. Being chosen is what makes a bond worth anything, and choosing is the only way anything gets truly joined."] },
    reading: { label: "As a reading", paras: ["The Lovers marks a crossroads, and rarely a casual one. A relationship arriving at its define-it-or-drift moment. A partnership whose halves pull in different directions. An offer that fits your plans but argues with your values. Its counsel is to choose with the deepest loyalty you have, because a choice made against what you love buys a future that will never quite fit.", "For the choosing itself, the advice is to ask what you actually love, in the order you actually love it, and let that ranking make the call. Wholeheartedness is the only currency this card accepts; a half-yes spends like a no. As for union, think of two instruments tuned to each other. Neither stops being itself. Together they make a sound neither could rehearse alone."] },
  },
  chariot: {
    surface: "The Guidebook",
    keywords: ["Victory", "Determination", "Willpower", "Control over opposing forces", "Ambition", "Momentum", "Focus", "Harnessing energy"],
    meaning: { label: "What the card means", paras: ["The Chariot is the deck's forward gear. It stands for victory, determination, and the control it takes to drive two pulling forces in one chosen direction. Other cards ask you to wait, reflect, or accept. The Chariot asks you to steer, and it fully expects you to arrive.", "Drawing it means something in your life has entered its push. Progress is available now in a way it was not a month ago, and the main variable left is your grip on the reins. The card is frank about the tension: real momentum almost always involves opposing pulls, speed against control, ambition against doubt. Driving is the act of holding both."] },
    reading: { label: "As a reading", paras: ["The Chariot rolls in where determination is being asked for by name. A goal at the stage where effort visibly compounds. A conflict between two drives that both claim the wheel. A stretch of resistance that is one hard week from breaking. Its counsel is to commit to the destination out loud, because a stated finish line recruits everything in you that was still deciding.", "On technique, the card is specific: firm on direction, relaxed on everything else. White-knuckling every detail spends strength the finish line will ask for, and reins were never meant to strangle. Correct course in small movements, and keep the horses fed. Control, in this card, means everything powerful in you finally pulling in the same direction."] },
  },
  strength: {
    surface: "The Guidebook",
    keywords: ["Inner strength", "Courage", "Patience", "Compassion", "Discipline", "Influence over force"],
    meaning: { label: "What the card means", paras: ["Strength is the card of power held gently. It stands for courage, patience, and the discipline that persuades rather than forces. The muscle here is entirely the quiet kind: where The Chariot wins by driving, Strength wins by settling the animal down without raising its voice. It is the deck's kindest picture of power.", "Drawing it means something is testing your composure rather than your capability. There is a force in the picture, a temper, an appetite, a fear, a difficult person, and the card is clear about the winning approach: steadiness. What resists pressure often responds to patience. It also insists that the strength in question is already yours, built from nothing more exotic than nerve held quietly over time."] },
    reading: { label: "As a reading", paras: ["Strength gathers around situations that force cannot fix. A conversation that will go exactly as well as your temper allows. A habit that laughs at willpower but yields to routine. A frightened person, possibly you, who needs calm more than solutions. Its counsel is to lower your voice at the moment raising it would feel best, and watch what that does.", "Its advice is to trust the soft approach for longer than feels natural, since gentleness tends to get abandoned a week before it would have worked. Courage here looks like staying: steady, unhurried, and unimpressed by the noise, until the situation stops bracing against you. Everyone who has ever gentled a wild thing knows the secret, and the secret was never the whip."] },
  },
  wheel_of_fortune: {
    surface: "The Guidebook",
    keywords: ["Luck", "Cycles", "Fate", "Turning points", "Ups and downs", "Opportunity"],
    meaning: { label: "What the card means", paras: ["The Wheel of Fortune is the card of the turn. It stands for luck, cycles, and the larger rotation that carries situations up, over, and around whether anyone consents or not. It is the deck's honest admission that some forces are bigger than planning. Fortunes rise, fall, and rise again on a timetable nobody is shown, and this card marks a moment when the timetable moves.", "Drawing it means a turning point has arrived or is close. Circumstances that held one shape for a long time are being reshuffled, and luck, that unfashionable word, is genuinely in play. It asks only that you loosen your grip on the version of events you had planned, because seasons change on their own authority, and nobody has ever argued the weather into different behavior."] },
    reading: { label: "As a reading", paras: ["The Wheel marks the exact spots where effort and outcome disconnect. A break that arrived from nowhere. A long unlucky stretch that is quietly running out. A door that opened the same week another closed. Its counsel is readiness rather than credit-taking or blame: position yourself where the good turn can reach you, and answer quickly when it does.", "The Wheel's advice is to think in seasons rather than verdicts. What it takes, it tends to return in another form, and what it brings deserves to be used rather than audited for worthiness. Timing is this card's whole department; deserving is filed somewhere else entirely. So spend the good turn while it is here. Luck that goes unclaimed simply rolls on to the next open hand."] },
  },
  justice: {
    surface: "The Guidebook",
    keywords: ["Fairness", "Truth", "Cause and effect", "Balance", "Accountability", "Clarity", "Impartiality"],
    meaning: { label: "What the card means", paras: ["Justice is the card of cause and effect. It stands for fairness, truth, accountability, and the long ledger in which everything is eventually counted. It is the least mystical card in the deck. Where the Wheel of Fortune deals in luck, Justice deals in consequence: every outcome has a cause, and the cause, on inspection, was usually a choice.", "Drawing it means a situation is moving toward its fair result. Something is being weighed: a decision, a dispute, an imbalance that has run longer than anyone admits. The card's promise cuts in both directions, because it deals in what is deserved rather than what is hoped. If you have been carrying more than your share, the tilt corrects in your favor. If a corner was cut, true it up before the measuring starts."] },
    reading: { label: "As a reading", paras: ["Justice turns up where an evening-out is due. A matter awaiting someone's decision. A pattern of giving that has not been returning. A consequence, welcome or not, arriving exactly on time. Its counsel is clean hands and clear records: act as if everything will be reviewed, because under this card it will be.", "Justice asks you to be the fairest actor in the room, starting with the account you keep of yourself. Honesty about your own part is the entry fee, and it buys something valuable: a verdict you can stand behind without flinching. Balance, in the end, is kept the way good books are kept, entry by entry, until the columns agree."] },
  },
  hanged_man: {
    surface: "The Guidebook",
    keywords: ["Suspension", "Pause", "Surrender", "Letting go", "Sacrifice", "Limbo", "Patience", "Seeing things differently"],
    meaning: { label: "What the card means", paras: ["The Hanged Man is the card of the deliberate pause. It stands for suspension, surrender, and the strange usefulness of a stretch where nothing can be pushed forward. The figure hangs upside down by choice, and that detail is the whole card: this is a stop taken on purpose, or one that becomes purposeful once accepted.", "Drawing it means some part of life is in suspension, and pushing is not moving the date. The card's instruction is to stop treating the pause as a malfunction, because limbo has work of its own. Hung in place, a person ends up seeing the situation from an angle effort never allows, and that view is the point of the whole delay."] },
    reading: { label: "As a reading", paras: ["The Hanged Man settles over everything that refuses to be hurried. An answer that will come when it comes. A recovery on its own calendar. A plan parked while the world catches up. Its counsel is surrender of the practical kind: since the wait cannot be shortened, stop paying for it twice, once in time and again in frustration.", "Its deeper advice is to use the stillness rather than merely endure it. Ask what the situation looks like when you are not managing it, because some truths only visit people who have stopped struggling. Willing sacrifice runs through this card too: something small is usually given up to hold the position. Surrender it gracefully. Fruit hangs upside down while it ripens, and it is not wasting the time."] },
  },
  temperance: {
    surface: "The Guidebook",
    keywords: ["Balance", "Moderation", "Patience", "Blending", "Alchemy", "Integration", "Finding the middle path", "Purpose"],
    meaning: { label: "What the card means", paras: ["Temperance is the card of the perfect mix. It stands for balance, moderation, and the patient blending of things that were never supposed to work together. Its skill is proportion: not too much, not too little, poured back and forth until the parts stop being parts. Of all the deck's virtues, this is the least showy and the hardest to actually do.", "Drawing it means something in your life is asking for proportion rather than force. Two demands, two moods, or two loyalties want integrating, and the winning move is a measured one: some of each, in the right ratio, adjusted slowly. The card has no interest in extremes, and that is a stance, not a shrug. Anyone can pour everything out. Knowing exactly how much takes the steadier hand."] },
    reading: { label: "As a reading", paras: ["Temperance drifts toward the places where balance has slipped. A work life and a home life negotiating for the same hours. An appetite that has been running the schedule. A conflict where both sides hold a piece of the right answer. Its counsel is adjustment over overhaul: small corrections, made early and often, beat the dramatic swing every time.", "Temperance asks you to keep faith with proportion even when the extremes look more decisive. The blend is where the skill shows: patience mixed into ambition, honesty tempered with kindness, rest folded into effort until the whole thing holds. Do the mixing slowly and taste as you go. Nobody ever built a lasting anything from a single ingredient."] },
  },
  devil: {
    surface: "The Guidebook",
    keywords: ["Temptation", "Bondage", "Addiction", "Obsession", "Materialism", "The shadow self", "Pleasure traps", "Raw energy", "Humor about flaws"],
    meaning: { label: "What the card means", paras: ["The Devil is the card of temptation and of every chain a person fastens on themselves. It stands for appetite, obsession, and the comfortable arrangements that quietly run the show: the habit, the luxury, the pattern that started as pleasure and stayed as management. The figure on the card holds people in loose chains, and the loose part matters more than the chains do.", "Drawing it means something has more of your leash than you usually admit, and the card wants the admission more than the repentance. It is honest about the mechanics: the binding here is voluntary, renewed daily, and dressed as reward. That same honesty is the way out. A chain you fastened yourself comes with your own hand already on the clasp."] },
    reading: { label: "As a reading", paras: ["The Devil grins from inside the pattern you already know about. A habit that answers to nobody. A purchase, a person, or a pleasure with more pull than it should have. An old coping trick that outlived what it was coping with. Its counsel is precision: name the attachment out loud, without theatrics, because a named appetite loses its favorite hiding place.", "The card's advice arrives with a wink, having no patience for denial or for self-flagellation either. It treats enjoyment as innocent and being managed as the entire case: keep the pleasures, fire the manager. Laugh at your flaws while you take their inventory, since solemnity has a poor record against habits. And note what the picture shows. The chains are loose enough to lift off, which makes staying a choice too."] },
  },
  star: {
    surface: "The Guidebook",
    keywords: ["Hope", "Inspiration", "Renewal", "Serenity", "Spiritual guidance", "Generosity", "A sense of purpose", "Optimism after hardship"],
    meaning: { label: "What the card means", paras: ["The Star is the card of hope with evidence. It stands for renewal, serenity, and the quiet that follows a hard stretch once the sky has finally cleared. It sits directly after The Tower in the deck, and the placement is the meaning: after the collapse, a calm, wide-open night, and the first believable reasons to look up again.", "Drawing it means the worst of something is over, and a gentler season is opening, provided you let it be gentle. Relief can be hard to trust after a long strain; the card suggests trusting it anyway. Nothing needs forcing now, and very little needs deciding. Healing, under this card, happens on its own once the pressure stops. The only assignment is to stop supplying the pressure."] },
    reading: { label: "As a reading", paras: ["The Star rises over whatever you have just come through. A crisis that has finally exhausted itself. A wound at the itching stage of mending. A hope you shelved during the hard part and can now take down again. Its counsel is replenishment: rest, water, small honest pleasures, and no sudden ambitions until the reserves are actually back.", "The Star's advice is generosity, especially toward yourself, because generosity is what surplus does, and you are being refilled. Share what the hard stretch taught you; someone a few steps behind needs exactly that. Keep one small light in view, a purpose, a person, a promise, and steer by it without hurrying toward it. Stars were made for navigating, and navigation only asks that you keep them in sight."] },
  },
  sun: {
    surface: "The Guidebook",
    keywords: ["Joy", "Success", "Optimism", "Vitality", "Warmth", "Clarity", "Enlightenment", "Being your true self", "Simple pleasures"],
    meaning: { label: "What the card means", paras: ["The Sun is the deck's card of plain joy. It stands for success, vitality, warmth, and things being every bit as good as they look. Where The Moon deals in shadows and maybes, The Sun deals in daylight. What shines under this card is real, and the goodness on display is the whole story.", "Drawing it means some part of life is simply working, and the card's first instruction is to notice. Enjoyment takes more discipline than it gets credit for: a good thing can be inspected until the shine wears off, and this card asks you to skip the inspection. Success is allowed to be uncomplicated here. Permission is most of the message, and it is granted."] },
    reading: { label: "As a reading", paras: ["The Sun lights up whatever deserves celebrating right now. A win that came in clean. A body or a mood returning to strength. A person whose company asks nothing and gives plenty. Its counsel is to be present for the good day while it is happening, since joy postponed for a safer moment has a way of expiring unclaimed.", "The Sun's other request is that you let yourself be seen enjoying it. Playing happiness down protects nothing and dims the room, warmth being the one resource that grows by the spending. Be the easy company today. Say the kind thing out loud, take the walk in the actual sunshine, and let simple be enough. Children understand this card instinctively. The whole trick of it is that there is no trick."] },
  },
  judgement: {
    surface: "The Guidebook",
    keywords: ["Reckoning", "Rebirth", "The inner calling", "Absolution", "Awakening", "A second chance", "A new purpose", "Evaluating the past"],
    meaning: { label: "What the card means", paras: ["Judgement is the card of the reckoning and the rebirth that follows it. It stands for the moment the past asks to be reviewed, a call that has to be answered, and the clean strength that comes from finally answering. The name sounds like a courtroom, but the verdict this card most often deals in is absolution.", "Drawing it means something unfinished is resurfacing, and this time it wants settling rather than another burial. An old ambition, an old wound, an old version of you is calling for a decision. The card is firm about the direction: review the past honestly, keep what still rings true, forgive the rest, and rise. What answers the call gets to begin again, lighter, and it is meant to be you."] },
    reading: { label: "As a reading", paras: ["Judgement sounds its horn over the unresolved. A conversation that has waited years for its second half. A calling you talked yourself out of once. A self-assessment that is overdue and, secretly, already drafted. Its counsel is to answer rather than deliberate, because a call like this does not improve with screening.", "Conduct the review like a fair judge rather than a prosecutor; the point of this reckoning was never punishment. Grant the pardons, starting with your own. Take the lesson, leave the sentence, and step into whatever the calling asks of you, changed and unembarrassed about it. Second chances keep a short boarding window. This card is the sound of yours being announced."] },
  },
  world: {
    surface: "The Guidebook",
    keywords: ["Completion", "Integration", "Accomplishment", "Travel", "Unity", "Wholeness", "The end of a cycle"],
    meaning: { label: "What the card means", paras: ["The World is the deck's final card, and it means what a final card should: completion, wholeness, the moment a long arc actually closes. It stands for accomplishment with nothing missing from it, integration, and the rare feeling of standing inside a result you once could only describe.", "Drawing it means something big is completing, or has, and the completion deserves to be treated as an event rather than a checkbox. A degree, a move, a recovery, a project years in the making: whatever it is, all of its parts have finally agreed to be one thing. The card's insistence is on the pause. Stand in the finished thing, look around, and let it count before anything new is allowed to start the clock again."] },
    reading: { label: "As a reading", paras: ["The World waits at true endings, the finished kind. A goal crossing from in-progress to done. A cycle that has taught what it had to teach. A long effort that ends with nothing left owing. Its counsel is to close it consciously: say the thank-yous, file the lessons, and resist starting the sequel out of sheer momentum.", "Celebrate at full size, without deflecting; that is most of the instruction. Completion is rare, most things in a life stay partial, and that is exactly why this one should be marked properly. When the celebrating is done, notice what the finishing made of you, because that is the part you keep. A completed circle releases its traveler, one lap wiser, onto larger ground."] },
  },
  wands_01: {
    surface: "The Guidebook",
    keywords: ["A new spark", "Inspiration", "Potential", "Creative energy", "Taking initiative", "Enthusiasm", "Conception"],
    meaning: { label: "What the card means", paras: ["The Ace of Wands is the first card of the suit of fire, the family of the deck that governs passion, creativity, and drive. Every ace is a seed, and this one is the live spark: pure creative urge arriving before it has a plan, a shape, or even a name. All of that comes later. The spark's only job is to catch.", "Drawing it means an itch to make something has entered the room: an idea that showed up mid-shower, an enthusiasm you cannot fully explain, energy looking for a place to burn. The card asks for kindling rather than architecture. Do one small real thing with the spark today, because inspiration converts to momentum through action and evaporates through scheduling."] },
    reading: { label: "As a reading", paras: ["The Ace of Wands flares up around beginnings that feel physical. A creative urge with an actual pulse to it. An enthusiasm that arrived from nowhere this week. A first step that keeps tapping you on the shoulder. Its counsel is speed in miniature: strike while it is hot, but strike small, a sketch, a message, an hour, not a life plan.", "Above all, protect the flame from your own analysis. New fire is fragile: explain it too early, budget it too hard, or show it to the wrong committee, and it goes out looking reasonable the whole time. Feed it in private first. Wands begin everything; whether this one becomes a bonfire is next month's question, and lighting it is this week's."] },
  },
  wands_02: {
    surface: "The Guidebook",
    keywords: ["Planning", "Foresight", "Decision-making", "Initial progress", "Ambition", "Looking outward"],
    meaning: { label: "What the card means", paras: ["The Two of Wands is the planner's card of the fire suit. It stands for foresight, ambition, and the moment a spark from the Ace grows serious enough to deserve a strategy. The figure on the card holds a globe in one hand: the idea has outgrown the room it started in, and the question has become where to take it.", "Drawing it means you are standing between the decision to act and the acting itself, weighing a known comfort against an open horizon. The card respects the pause; foresight is genuine work, and it is being done. But it also sets a quiet deadline. Plans mature, and then they spoil, and what separates a vision from a daydream is a departure date."] },
    reading: { label: "As a reading", paras: ["The Two of Wands hovers over choices about scale. A venture that could stay safe or go wide. An ambition that has outgrown its current container. An early win asking whether it was a pilot or the whole program. Its counsel is to plan like a realist and choose like an ambitious one, because security rarely needs a champion and possibility always does.", "Its advice is to look outward deliberately. Study whoever has already done the bigger version, price the actual risk instead of the imagined one, and notice how much of the safety being protected is familiarity wearing a uniform. Then pick a direction while options still exist. The advantage of high ground is choosing a route before the road starts choosing for you."] },
  },
  wands_03: {
    surface: "The Guidebook",
    keywords: ["Expansion", "Foresight realized", "Initial success", "Waiting for results", "Commerce", "Progress", "Looking ahead with confidence"],
    meaning: { label: "What the card means", paras: ["The Three of Wands is the card of plans afloat. It stands for expansion, first returns, and the stretch after a commitment when results begin arriving from a distance. Where the Two of Wands still weighs the venture, the Three has already launched: the ships are out, and the first of them are turning home.", "Drawing it means something you set in motion is starting to answer. Early results are in, and they are shaped like encouragement: proof that the idea travels, that the effort carries, that the bigger version is not a fantasy. The card marks this as a waypoint rather than a finish. What has come back so far is confirmation. The full return is still at sea, and it is worth waiting in confidence rather than in worry."] },
    reading: { label: "As a reading", paras: ["The Three of Wands looks out from wherever effort has begun paying. A first client or first yes from beyond the usual circle. A skill starting to open doors it was built for. An investment of time showing its first real interest. Its counsel is patience of the active kind: keep the operation running while the long results travel, and widen the plan now that it is proven.", "As for growth, scale by decision rather than by drift. Extend the route while momentum is real, hand off what has become routine, and let the evidence talk you into the bigger version you were too modest to draft. Trust travels. What you send out over the water comes back with interest, and the boats never launched are the only ones guaranteed empty."] },
  },
  wands_04: {
    surface: "The Guidebook",
    keywords: ["Celebration", "Harmony", "Homecoming", "Stability achieved", "Community", "Completion of a phase", "Joy", "Milestones", "Marriage"],
    meaning: { label: "What the card means", paras: ["The Four of Wands is the fire suit's first celebration. It stands for milestones, homecoming, and the stability that follows a stretch of real effort: the frame of the thing is up, and the people who helped are gathering. Fours in this deck are resting points, and this is the happiest of them.", "Drawing it means something has reached a stage worth marking. A foundation has held, a phase has closed cleanly, and the fitting response is a gathering rather than more work. The card is insistent about this: celebration is structural. Milestones left unmarked quietly stop feeling like progress, and the people who built the thing with you need the party as much as you do."] },
    reading: { label: "As a reading", paras: ["The Four of Wands goes up when the good news is real. A move that has become a home. A project crossing its first true threshold. A relationship settling into something worth announcing. Its counsel is to stop and mark the moment, out loud and in company, before the next phase starts demanding the attention.", "Note who the card gathers: this is a communal celebration, never a solo one. Stability worth having is stability shared, and the surest measure of a foundation is how many people feel safe standing on it. So open the doors and make the invitation wide. What got built becomes a home at exactly the moment somebody else is welcomed into it."] },
  },
  wands_05: {
    surface: "The Guidebook",
    keywords: ["Conflict", "Competition", "Disagreement", "Minor struggles", "Clashing egos", "Dynamic tension"],
    meaning: { label: "What the card means", paras: ["The Five of Wands is the fire suit's friction. It stands for competition, clashing egos, and the noisy stage where several strong energies want the same space. Fives in this deck mark the rough patch after stability, and this one is the most physical: a scuffle, not a war, with more sparks than injuries.", "Drawing it means you are in a contested moment. Ideas are colliding, personalities are jostling, and everything takes three rounds of argument longer than it should. The card reads the mess generously: this much friction means this many people care. Competition at this stage is how the strongest version of the idea gets found, provided nobody starts mistaking the sparring for the enemy."] },
    reading: { label: "As a reading", paras: ["The Five of Wands breaks out around crowded ambitions. A team full of leads and short on followers. A field where every rival showed up sharp. A debate that keeps reigniting because both sides are half right. Its counsel is to stay in the scrimmage without turning it personal, because the friction is doing work the agreement never could.", "Treat the contest as training, which is what the card shows: five figures sparring, nobody bleeding. A good rival finds the weak points while the stakes are still low, and that is a favor, however it feels. Hold your position where it matters, yield where it does not, and watch what the collision teaches. These fives usually resolve the same way: five solo acts discovering they are one crew."] },
  },
  wands_06: {
    surface: "The Guidebook",
    keywords: ["Public recognition", "Victory", "Acclaim", "Success", "Confidence", "Progress", "Leadership after struggle"],
    meaning: { label: "What the card means", paras: ["The Six of Wands is the fire suit's victory parade. It stands for success made public: the win is real, the crowd knows about it, and the recognition has finally caught up with the work. After the scuffle of the Five, the Six is the moment somebody rides out in front with the crowd behind them.", "Drawing it means acclaim is arriving, or is overdue and moving. Something you did is finally being seen, and the card wants the moment received well, without deflection and without suspicion of the applause. Recognition is fuel for the next stretch of the road, and it partly belongs to the people who backed you. Accepting it gracefully pays both debts at once."] },
    reading: { label: "As a reading", paras: ["The Six of Wands rides through wherever effort has gone public. A win with your name attached this time. Praise landing from a direction you respect. A comeback that outran everyone's quiet doubts. Its counsel is to stand in the moment fully, because received recognition steadies confidence, and deflected recognition teaches people to stop offering it.", "The card's second lesson is what victory is for. Use the visibility while it is warm: ask for the bigger stage, the harder project, the trust that yesterday's win just purchased. And spend some of the credit publicly on the people who were in the fight with you. A parade is the one vehicle that gets larger the more passengers it takes on."] },
  },
  wands_07: {
    surface: "The Guidebook",
    keywords: ["Standing your ground", "Defense", "Conviction", "Perseverance", "Maintaining position", "Challenge"],
    meaning: { label: "What the card means", paras: ["The Seven of Wands is the fire suit's defense of the summit. It stands for conviction under pressure: a position earned, then challenged, then held. Where the Six enjoyed the crowd's approval, the Seven discovers the crowd contains challengers, and the figure on the card plants its feet anyway.", "Drawing it means something you have built or claimed is being contested: a stance, a standard, a piece of ground that others suddenly want. The card is realistic about the cost and firmly on your side about the holding. Position invites challenge; that is what being visible means. What matters now is that you actually believe in what you are defending, because conviction is the entire supply line."] },
    reading: { label: "As a reading", paras: ["The Seven of Wands digs in wherever your ground is being tested. A decision under fresh second-guessing. A standard you keep having to re-justify. Competitors crowding a space you cleared first. Its counsel is to defend the essential and only the essential, since defending everything with equal heat is the fastest way to hold nothing.", "Check the foundation before the fight, and then fight without apology. A position worth holding survives your own honest audit first; once it has, stop relitigating it every time someone raises a hand. Answer the strongest challenge, ignore the noise, and let endurance do the arguing. A summit is rarely taken from the person who genuinely wants to be standing on it."] },
  },
  wands_08: {
    surface: "The Guidebook",
    keywords: ["Speed", "Action", "Movement", "Swift change", "Communication", "Travel", "Things falling into place", "Quick decisions"],
    meaning: { label: "What the card means", paras: ["The Eight of Wands is the fire suit at full speed. It stands for swift movement, news in flight, and the sudden alignment that lets stalled things happen all at once. It is the deck's purest card of motion: eight shafts already in the air, every one of them about to land.", "Drawing it means the pace of your situation is about to change. Answers arrive in clusters, decisions that idled for months resolve in days, and travel or news crosses the distance fast. The card's request is readiness rather than reflection; this is a poor week for long deliberation and a great one for clearing the runway. When everything moves at once, the prepared simply say yes faster."] },
    reading: { label: "As a reading", paras: ["The Eight of Wands streaks through wherever momentum has been waiting. A reply that starts an avalanche of them. A green light that turns three other lights green. A window that opened suddenly and will close the same way. Its counsel is to move with the speed rather than against it, and save the fine-tuning for the landing.", "Ride it lightly. Speed like this rewards short messages, quick decisions, and trust in the aim you took before things accelerated; mid-flight is a bad altitude for redesigning the arrow. Keep the days uncluttered, answer while the answers are hot, and let some things be fast instead of perfect. Slow seasons return soon enough. This week, the wind is a gift."] },
  },
  wands_09: {
    surface: "The Guidebook",
    keywords: ["Resilience", "Persistence", "The last stand", "Weary defense", "Boundaries", "Guardedness", "Strength in reserve", "Nearing completion"],
    meaning: { label: "What the card means", paras: ["The Nine of Wands is the fire suit's last watch. It stands for resilience near the finish: bandaged, guarded, still upright, with eight fights already absorbed and one stretch left to hold. Nines in this deck sit one step from completion, and this one asks what a person does when almost-done feels like one round too many.", "Drawing it means you are closer to the end than your energy suggests, and the card holds both truths at once. The weariness is legitimate: this has cost something, and the guardedness shows it. So is the position: the line has held every single time it was tested. The card's arithmetic is simple and kind. Anyone still standing this late is far more durable than they currently feel."] },
    reading: { label: "As a reading", paras: ["The Nine of Wands leans on the doorframe of nearly-finished things. A long project in its last, heaviest mile. A commitment tested often enough to make cynicism tempting. A boundary that needs holding one more time. Its counsel is to guard the essentials and spend nothing extra, because the reserves have a job left to do.", "Rest counts as strategy now. Patch what is patchable, decline every optional battle, and be suspicious of the voice claiming it was all for nothing; exhaustion is a bad historian. Then hold. The difference between defeat and triumph, this late, is usually one more held morning. The card's figure is tired, marked, and unbeaten, and the third word is the one that counts."] },
  },
  wands_10: {
    surface: "The Guidebook",
    keywords: ["Burden", "Responsibility", "Overload", "Hard work", "Stress", "Taking on too much", "A heavy completion"],
    meaning: { label: "What the card means", paras: ["The Ten of Wands is the fire suit's full armload. It stands for burden, responsibility, and the strange arithmetic of success: every yes ever said, now being carried at once, uphill, by one person. Tens in this deck are completions, and this one completes with a heavy step: the goal is close, and so is the limit.", "Drawing it means the load is real and mostly self-assembled. Somewhere along the way, capability became an invitation: you could carry more, so more arrived, and the habit of accepting it started passing for a personality. The card does not doubt your strength. It questions the sorting. Some of what is on your back is yours, some is momentum, and some belongs to people perfectly capable of carrying it."] },
    reading: { label: "As a reading", paras: ["The Ten of Wands bends under whatever cannot be put down gracefully. A role that quietly swallowed three others. A season where every task is somehow the urgent one. A finish line visible but arriving through mud. Its counsel is triage without guilt: finish what is truly yours, hand back what never was, and stop auditioning for sainthood.", "Put things down in public. Quiet overload reads as competence from the outside, so the help never comes because the strain never shows; say the load out loud and watch how much of it turns out to be movable. Delegation, this card insists, is a completion skill, the thing that separates finishing the journey from becoming its pack animal. Arrive lighter. What counts at the door is the delivery, and nobody grades the carrying."] },
  },
  wands_11: {
    surface: "The Guidebook",
    keywords: ["A messenger of inspiration", "Enthusiasm", "Exploration", "The spark of a new idea", "Creative beginnings", "A free spirit", "Discovery"],
    meaning: { label: "What the card means", paras: ["The Page of Wands is the fire suit's youngest face. Pages in this deck are students and messengers, each carrying their suit's energy in its rawest form, and this one carries the spark: enthusiasm without portfolio, curiosity with its shoes already on. Nothing about the Page is finished, and nothing about it is jaded either.", "Drawing it means a fresh enthusiasm is asking for room, and its inexperience is a feature. You are a beginner again somewhere, or could be, and the card is delighted about it: beginners notice what experts have stopped seeing, and they try what experts have talked themselves out of. Give the new interest some unstructured time before assigning it a purpose. Messages this young mostly need to be heard out."] },
    reading: { label: "As a reading", paras: ["The Page of Wands bounds in wherever curiosity has been sparking. A subject you keep reading about past bedtime for no career reason. An invitation toward somewhere unfamiliar. A creative itch with no credentials and real persistence. Its counsel is to follow the interest as an amateur on purpose, because amateur is just the old word for someone who does it for love.", "Explore without a syllabus. Take the class, book the detour, ask the naive question in the room full of veterans, and let the enthusiasm be visible; sparks travel between people, and yours will find its senders and receivers. Not every flare has to become a vocation. The Page's whole job is delivering the message, and the message, here, is simply: this way looks fun, come see."] },
  },
  wands_12: {
    surface: "The Guidebook",
    keywords: ["Action", "Adventure", "Impulsiveness", "Energy", "Passion", "Charisma", "Charging ahead", "Travel", "Hastiness"],
    meaning: { label: "What the card means", paras: ["The Knight of Wands is the fire suit at a gallop. Knights in this deck are their suit's energy taken to the extreme, and this one is pure charge: passion, adventure, and momentum with the brakes considered optional. It is the deck's most exciting arrival and its least careful, usually in the same afternoon.", "Drawing it means a surge of bold energy is moving through your situation, yours or somebody else's. Things are catching fire in the good sense: plans accelerate, doors get pushed on, and charisma is doing real work. The card backs the boldness and posts one small warning next to it. Speed of this kind finishes gloriously or leaves early, and the difference is usually whether the energy ever picked a direction."] },
    reading: { label: "As a reading", paras: ["The Knight of Wands kicks the door in wherever boldness is due. A plan you have been over-preparing that mostly needs launching. An adventure with a short RSVP window. A charismatic arrival, in your life or in your mirror, promising motion. Its counsel is to use the surge while it lasts, and to aim before spurring.", "Channel it, and it is unstoppable; indulge it, and it is a highlight reel of unfinished starts. Pick the one quest that deserves this much horsepower and give it the whole animal. Let the passion set the pace and let something steadier set the route. Then go. The Knight's virtue is that it actually leaves, today, while the rest of the world is still drafting the plan."] },
  },
  wands_13: {
    surface: "The Guidebook",
    keywords: ["Confidence", "Warmth", "Vibrancy", "Determination", "Social grace", "Independence", "Courage", "Magnetism", "Leadership"],
    meaning: { label: "What the card means", paras: ["The Queen of Wands is the fire suit worn as presence. Queens in this deck have made their suit's energy fully their own, and this one radiates it: confidence, warmth, and a vitality that other people can feel from across the room. Hers is fire as hearth and beacon at once, sustaining her own and visible for miles.", "Drawing her means the moment calls for owned confidence, yours or a figure like her nearby. Something wants doing in the open, with warmth and full wattage, by someone unafraid to be seen caring. The card considers self-assurance a renewable resource: it feeds on honest use. Whatever you have been doing at half-brightness to keep the room comfortable, she would like turned up."] },
    reading: { label: "As a reading", paras: ["The Queen of Wands presides wherever warmth and will share a chair. A leadership moment that needs heart as much as spine. A gathering that comes alive once somebody warm takes charge of it. A talent you have been dimming out of politeness. Her counsel is to bring the full flame and let the room adjust, because it will.", "Pair the shine with spine; that is her whole curriculum. Warmth without boundaries becomes service to every passing demand, so keep the independence that makes generosity a choice. Encourage loudly, decide firmly, and hold court without apologizing for the wattage. Fires this steady are rare, and their real work is what they make possible for others: people think better, dare more, and warm up quickly in the company of someone burning well."] },
  },
  wands_14: {
    surface: "The Guidebook",
    keywords: ["Leadership", "Vision", "Inspiration", "Charismatic authority", "Entrepreneurship", "Bold action", "Mastery of passion", "The big picture"],
    meaning: { label: "What the card means", paras: ["The King of Wands is the fire suit in command. Kings in this deck are their suit's energy matured into authority, and this one turned passion into an enterprise: vision, leadership, and the confidence to point at a horizon and make the pointing stick. The fire still burns; it simply answers to him now.", "Drawing him means the moment wants big-picture nerve, from you or from someone whose lead you are weighing. Something has outgrown day-to-day management and needs a direction worth years, declared with enough conviction that others can build careers inside it. The card's faith is specific: passion, seasoned and aimed, is the most durable authority there is. People follow heat that knows where it is going."] },
    reading: { label: "As a reading", paras: ["The King of Wands holds council wherever vision needs an owner. A venture ready to scale past its founder's hands. A team that has plenty of tasks and no story. A decision that needs somebody to simply call it. Its counsel is to take the long view out loud, because groups drift without a declared horizon and rally fast behind one.", "Lead like the fire is a resource, and delegate everything but the vision. The King does his real work at the level of direction: choosing the mountain, naming the pace, and letting abler climbers own the ropes. Keep the founder's spark visible; it is the asset everything else was built on. Bold action, at this rank, mostly means deciding big and standing by it in public."] },
  },
  cups_01: {
    surface: "The Guidebook",
    keywords: ["Overflowing love", "New emotional beginnings", "Compassion", "Receptivity", "Joy", "Intuition sparked", "Spiritual connection", "Creativity"],
    meaning: { label: "What the card means", paras: ["The Ace of Cups opens the suit of water, the family of the deck that governs emotion, relationship, intuition, and the heart's kind of creativity. Every ace is a seed, and this one is planted in the chest: a new feeling, a fresh openness, the first swell of something that wants to overflow.", "Drawing it means the heart is being offered a beginning: a connection sparking, a compassion rising, a creative feeling arriving ahead of any plan for it. The card asks for receptivity above all. New feeling this fresh does not need vetting, defining, or forecasting yet; it needs to be let in. Cups begin by being held open, and everything else the suit promises follows from that."] },
    reading: { label: "As a reading", paras: ["The Ace of Cups wells up wherever feeling is newly arriving. A person you find yourself hoping will call. A warmth toward someone that surprised you. A creative pull that feels more like affection than ambition. Its counsel is openness without agenda: let the feeling be real for a while before deciding what it is for.", "Receive first and sort later. A heart at a beginning is allowed to be generous past all reason, and this card would rather see a little spill than a sealed cup. Express the warmth while it is fresh: say the kind thing, start the tender project, answer softness with softness. Overflow is the design, and what overflows is exactly the part that reaches other people."] },
  },
  cups_02: {
    surface: "The Guidebook",
    keywords: ["Union", "Partnership", "Mutual attraction", "Connection", "Shared values", "Harmony", "Truce"],
    meaning: { label: "What the card means", paras: ["The Two of Cups is the water suit's meeting of eyes. It stands for partnership, mutual attraction, and the moment two people recognize something in each other and decide it matters. Where The Lovers weighs a life-sized choice, the Two of Cups is smaller and nearer: one connection, forming right now, in both directions at once.", "Drawing it means a bond in your life is at the exchange stage, where what is offered and what is returned are finding their balance, and the card's happy report is that the flow runs both ways. Mutuality is its entire subject: the small daily trade of attention and regard that every larger union is secretly made of. Tend the trade and the union tends itself."] },
    reading: { label: "As a reading", paras: ["The Two of Cups passes between people wherever a real connection is forming. A friendship crossing into trusted territory. A working relationship that has started finishing each other's ideas. An attraction that is, by every visible sign, mutual. Its counsel is reciprocity in the small things, because bonds are built at the scale of returned messages, not declarations.", "Meet the offer at its level. Match confidences with confidences, effort with effort, and honesty with the kind of honesty that costs something; symmetry is what makes a bond feel safe enough to deepen. And guard the truce clauses: repair small ruptures quickly, while they are still small. Two cups held level is the whole picture, and level is a verb here."] },
  },
  cups_03: {
    surface: "The Guidebook",
    keywords: ["Joyful gatherings", "Friendship", "Community", "Collaboration", "Celebration", "Shared happiness", "Harvest", "Abundance"],
    meaning: { label: "What the card means", paras: ["The Three of Cups is the water suit's festival. It stands for friendship, community, and the specific joy that only exists in company: shared, loud, and larger with every person added. If the Two is a bond between two people, the Three is what happens when the bonds form a circle.", "Drawing it means your people are the point right now. Something deserves celebrating together, a connection wants renewing, or joy has simply been running too solitary lately. The card's claim is cheerfully practical: happiness compounds in company, and friendships are kept alive by exactly the gatherings that busy seasons cancel first."] },
    reading: { label: "As a reading", paras: ["The Three of Cups gets the group text going. A win that belongs to more than one name. A friendship overdue for an actual evening together. A community that would form instantly if somebody sent the invitation. Its counsel is to be the one who organizes it, because everyone is waiting for someone, and the card just made it you.", "Show up fully when you get there. Put the phone away, tell the old stories, celebrate each other's news like it is your own, and let the evening run long; collective joy is a skill that atrophies quietly and returns instantly. Note the card's timing, too. It tends to appear at harvests of one kind or another, and whatever you have been growing alone is ready to be shared."] },
  },
  cups_04: {
    surface: "The Guidebook",
    keywords: ["Apathy", "Contemplation", "Boredom", "Missed opportunities", "Withdrawal", "Discontent", "Re-evaluation", "Meditation"],
    meaning: { label: "What the card means", paras: ["The Four of Cups is the water suit gone flat. It stands for apathy, boredom, and the season when nothing on offer looks interesting, including some things that genuinely are. Fours in this deck are resting points, and this one has rested slightly too long: the pause has begun preserving the mood instead of the person.", "Drawing it means discontent has settled in somewhere, quiet and unglamorous. Options exist and are being waved away without real inspection, while the mind circles the same dissatisfactions like furniture it has stopped seeing. The card is patient with this, because contemplation is real work when it is actually contemplating. It draws one gentle line: there is a difference between sitting with a question and simply sitting, and only one of them ends."] },
    reading: { label: "As a reading", paras: ["The Four of Cups slumps wherever appetite has gone missing. An offer dismissed before it was examined. A routine that has smoothed every day into the same day. A restlessness with no plan attached to it. Its counsel is a deliberate second look at the thing most recently refused, since apathy grades opportunities poorly and rarely rechecks its work.", "Change the vantage before changing the verdict. Boredom is usually a stale angle rather than a barren field, and the cure is often embarrassingly small: a new room, a new question, one accepted invitation attended with actual attention. The card's famous detail is the fourth cup, offered from outside the frame while the figure studies the first three. Something is being extended to you right now. Looking up is the entire assignment."] },
  },
  cups_05: {
    surface: "The Guidebook",
    keywords: ["Loss", "Grief", "Regret", "Disappointment", "Sadness", "Disillusionment"],
    meaning: { label: "What the card means", paras: ["The Five of Cups is the water suit's grief. It stands for loss, regret, and the heavy stretch when what went wrong is the only thing the eye can find. Fives in this deck are its rough patches, and this one is the quietest: a figure in a dark cloak, three cups down, and the mourning still in progress.", "Drawing it means something has genuinely been lost, and the card refuses to hurry you past that. The spill was real, and the sadness is earned and allowed. But the picture holds a second fact with equal weight: two cups still stand, unspilled, just behind the mourner, and they never leave the frame. Grief narrows the vision without emptying the table. What remains is real too, and it is patient."] },
    reading: { label: "As a reading", paras: ["The Five of Cups stands vigil over disappointments that mattered. A plan that failed after real investment. A relationship that ended before its story did. A regret that keeps re-running its one scene. Its counsel is to mourn properly instead of efficiently, because sorrow given its due leaves on time, and sorrow rushed keeps returning for what it is owed.", "When the turn comes, and it will, make it physically. The card's mourner must literally turn around to see what survived, and so will you: name two things still standing, then walk toward one of them. The picture is honest in both directions. Three spilled and two standing is a real loss, and it is also, quietly, enough to build with."] },
  },
  cups_06: {
    surface: "The Guidebook",
    keywords: ["Nostalgia", "Childhood memories", "Simple joys", "Innocence", "Giving and receiving", "Gifts from the past", "Returning home", "Harmony"],
    meaning: { label: "What the card means", paras: ["The Six of Cups is the water suit's look homeward. It stands for nostalgia, childhood sweetness, and the gifts that travel forward out of the past: old friendships, family flavors, the person you were before life got complicated. It is the deck's gentlest card, and it plays fair: the past it shows is the part worth keeping.", "Drawing it means the past is visiting, kindly. A memory keeps surfacing, an old name reappears, or a simple pleasure from an earlier chapter is asking to be taken seriously again. The card treats nostalgia as a resource rather than a retreat: what you loved before you edited yourself is a reliable record of what you actually are. Visit, collect, and come back with something."] },
    reading: { label: "As a reading", paras: ["The Six of Cups knocks with something familiar in hand. A friend from an earlier era who still fits. A childhood pleasure you stopped granting yourself for no defensible reason. A family story that explains more than it used to. Its counsel is to accept the gift as a gift, without irony, because sweetness taken plainly is the whole point of this card.", "Reach backward on purpose. Send the message to the old friend, cook the childhood meal, take the detour past the old house, and give innocence a seat at the adult table; simple joys survive every sophistication we stack on top of them. Just leave the return ticket intact. The past makes a nourishing visit and a poor address, and this card, read rightly, is a visit."] },
  },
  cups_07: {
    surface: "The Guidebook",
    keywords: ["Choices", "Options", "Fantasy", "Illusion", "Wishful thinking", "Imagination", "Dreams", "Temptation", "Needing discernment"],
    meaning: { label: "What the card means", paras: ["The Seven of Cups is the water suit's hall of options. It stands for choices multiplying past usefulness: possibilities, fantasies, and wishes crowding one shelf, each glittering, few examined. Where The Moon blurs what is true, the Seven of Cups blurs what is wanted, and the fog in both cases is homegrown.", "Drawing it means you are standing in front of too many doors, and the abundance has quietly become the obstacle. Some of the options are real, some are decorated versions of avoidance, and daydreaming has started doing the work deciding should be doing. The card enjoys imagination and says so; the trouble is only that a wish, left unchosen, spends the same hours a plan does and pays out nothing."] },
    reading: { label: "As a reading", paras: ["The Seven of Cups shimmers over decisions that keep not happening. A list of possibilities that grows instead of shrinking. A fantasy so well-furnished it feels like progress. An open question kept open because open is comfortable. Its counsel is discernment with a deadline: examine each cup for substance, then choose while choosing still costs less than drifting.", "Test the contents, one at a time. For each option, ask what it demands on an ordinary Tuesday, because fantasies never include the Tuesdays and real futures are built of them. Then honor the imagination that produced the whole display; it is a gift misapplied, and once a single cup is chosen it becomes the engine again. Pick one dream and give it a calendar. The other six were only ever going to be window displays."] },
  },
  cups_08: {
    surface: "The Guidebook",
    keywords: ["Moving on", "Seeking deeper meaning", "Letting go", "Withdrawal", "Abandonment", "Disillusionment", "A journey for truth"],
    meaning: { label: "What the card means", paras: ["The Eight of Cups is the water suit's walking-away card. It stands for the decision to leave something that still works on paper: the arrangement is intact, the cups are neatly stacked, and the figure is already moving off into the hills. This is departure as integrity, chosen when enough stopped being the same as meaningful.", "Drawing it means part of you has already left something, and the rest of you is negotiating the timing. The card's honesty runs both directions: what you are leaving is genuinely fine, and fine is genuinely the reason to go. Walking away from wreckage takes no card at all. The hard, honorable departure is from the good-enough thing that quietly stopped feeding you, and this card exists to say it is allowed."] },
    reading: { label: "As a reading", paras: ["The Eight of Cups waits by the coat rack of finished chapters. A role that fits and no longer matters. A pursuit achieved all the way to hollow. A place, group, or version of yourself that you have outgrown without drama. Its counsel is to trust the quiet verdict, because meaning does not return to where it has stopped living, however politely you wait.", "Leave well. Settle what needs settling, thank what deserves thanking, and go without manufacturing a villain to justify the exit; some departures are nobody's fault and still necessary. Then walk toward the question rather than the answer, because this journey is a search, and searches begin unfinished on purpose. The card sends its traveler uphill, into quieter country. Deeper is that way."] },
  },
  cups_09: {
    surface: "The Guidebook",
    keywords: ["Wishes fulfilled", "Contentment", "Satisfaction", "Emotional fulfillment", "Comfort", "Sensual pleasure", "Abundance"],
    meaning: { label: "What the card means", paras: ["The Nine of Cups is the water suit's satisfaction, and the whole deck's wish card. It stands for contentment, comfort, and desires actually fulfilled: the table is set, the cups are arranged, and the host is beaming for good reason. Nines sit one step from completion, and this one already feels complete.", "Drawing it means a wish is being granted, or recently was, and the card's entire instruction is to actually enjoy it. Emotional fulfillment is the specialty here: comfort earned, pleasure arrived, a stretch of life where wanting and having briefly agree. Take the moment at face value. Wishes spend years being worked for and minutes being savored, and this card thinks the ratio is a scandal."] },
    reading: { label: "As a reading", paras: ["The Nine of Cups makes itself comfortable wherever satisfaction is available. A goal reached that still feels as good as advertised. A comfort you can finally afford, in money, time, or peace. A wish from years ago quietly granted since. Its counsel is savoring as an active verb: notice the having, out loud, to yourself, while it is happening.", "Enjoy it without cross-examining it. Sensual pleasure, good company, and simple comfort are the joys bodies were built for, and this card grants them without a means test. One caution rides along: satisfaction hoards poorly. Shared, it stays warm; guarded, it curdles into smugness on its own schedule. So set out more cups than you strictly need. A wish come true was always meant to have witnesses."] },
  },
  cups_10: {
    surface: "The Guidebook",
    keywords: ["Lasting happiness", "Family harmony", "Emotional completion", "Joy", "Community", "Shared blessings", "Happily ever after", "Peak emotional experience"],
    meaning: { label: "What the card means", paras: ["The Ten of Cups is the water suit's happy ending. It stands for lasting happiness: family harmony, shared blessings, the rainbow-over-the-roof picture of a life that worked. Tens in this deck are completions, and where the fire suit's ten arrives burdened, this one arrives home. The picture is complete, and it means to stay.", "Drawing it means the emotional account is full, or filling: the people are the right people, the home feels like one, and happiness has stopped being an event and started being a climate. The card asks for two things at once. Recognize the completeness while you are inside it, since these are the days future nostalgia will be made of, and hold it with open hands rather than a grip."] },
    reading: { label: "As a reading", paras: ["The Ten of Cups comes home to whole-hearted arrangements. A family, given or chosen, at genuine peace. A household that other people relax inside. A long love that kept its warmth through the sensible years. Its counsel is presence over improvement: stop renovating the moment and live in it.", "Say it out loud to the people in the picture. Lasting happiness runs on maintenance, gratitude spoken, rituals kept, small repairs made early, and the keeping is joyful work rather than overhead. Let the completeness loosen you, too; a full cup can afford generosity in every direction. The rainbow on this card is a promise caught in the act of holding."] },
  },
  cups_11: {
    surface: "The Guidebook",
    keywords: ["A messenger of feelings", "Intuitive messages", "Sensitivity", "Curiosity", "Creative beginnings", "Synchronicity", "The dreamer"],
    meaning: { label: "What the card means", paras: ["The Page of Cups is the water suit's dreamer. Pages are the deck's students and messengers, and this one studies feelings: a sensitive, curious soul who takes signals from everywhere, including dreams, hunches, and the fish that seems to speak from the cup. It is the most whimsical card in the deck, entirely on purpose.", "Drawing it means something tender and imaginative is trying to get your attention: an intuition, an artistic impulse, a feeling that arrived before its explanation. The card asks you to take the small strange signal seriously, since these messages come only once and quietly. Sensitivity, here, is an instrument rather than a fragility, and curiosity about your own currents is the whole skill being taught."] },
    reading: { label: "As a reading", paras: ["The Page of Cups surfaces with something odd and precious. A dream that lingered past breakfast. A creative urge with more feeling than plan. A coincidence that seems, insistently, addressed to you. Its counsel is to receive the message before judging the messenger, because whimsy is often insight wearing its off-duty clothes.", "Answer in kind. Write the idea down, make the small tender thing, tell someone the dream at the risk of sounding silly; expressiveness grows by being used, and so does the courage to show it. Synchronicity favors the people who acknowledge it, whatever the mechanism. Keep the cup held out in front of you, the way the Page does. Curious things land in open vessels."] },
  },
  cups_12: {
    surface: "The Guidebook",
    keywords: ["Romance", "Charm", "Imagination", "Following the heart", "Idealism", "The beauty seeker", "Artistic endeavors", "A messenger of love"],
    meaning: { label: "What the card means", paras: ["The Knight of Cups is the water suit riding out. Knights carry their suit to its extreme, and this one is romance in motion: a seeker of beauty, a maker of gestures, an idealist who follows feeling the way other knights follow orders. He is the deck's most charming arrival, and he means every word of it, at least today.", "Drawing it means the heart is proposing something: a pursuit, a courtship, a creative quest, an ideal worth dressing up for. The card sides with the gesture. Feelings gain reality by being expressed beautifully, and beauty itself is a legitimate destination rather than a detour. It adds one grounding note. Ideals travel best with provisions, so let the romance carry some follow-through in its saddlebags."] },
    reading: { label: "As a reading", paras: ["The Knight of Cups presents himself wherever feeling wants form. An affection ready to be declared properly. A creative vision asking for a devoted season. A cause, person, or place that has quietly become your ideal. Its counsel is to make the gesture wholehearted and concrete, because half-sent love letters do nothing at either end.", "Follow the beauty with your eyes open. Chasing what moves you is a compass, and compasses still need roads: pair the invitation with a date, the vision with a first draft, the ideal with one unglamorous step. Keep the charm honest by letting it survive the errands. A grand gesture that shows up twice stops being a gesture. It starts being a devotion."] },
  },
  cups_13: {
    surface: "The Guidebook",
    keywords: ["Emotional maturity", "Compassion", "Empathy", "Intuition", "Nurturing", "A calm presence", "The counselor", "The healer"],
    meaning: { label: "What the card means", paras: ["The Queen of Cups is the water suit held steady. Queens embody their suit as presence, and hers is emotional depth that has learned its own tides: compassion, empathy, and a stillness that makes other people's feelings safe to have. She is the one the deck sends when someone needs to be truly heard.", "Drawing her means the moment calls for emotional intelligence of the quiet kind, in you or from someone near you. Something wants tending rather than solving: a person mid-storm, a feeling that needs naming, a room that needs one unhurried presence. The card's teaching is that receptivity is a strength with a posture. Held steadily, other people's storms pass through without becoming your own."] },
    reading: { label: "As a reading", paras: ["The Queen of Cups keeps the kettle warm wherever feelings need company. A friend who needs listening, not fixing. A conversation that keeps stalling at the surface. Your own emotions, overdue for an honest sit-down. Her counsel is presence before advice, because most hearts already know their answer and mostly need a witness.", "Care with edges. Empathy without boundaries floods, and the Queen never floods: she feels fully, reflects honestly, and stays herself the entire time. Offer that to the people you love, and insist on receiving it too. Refill privately, in quiet hours nobody has to approve. The waters she rules reward the composed, and composure, at this depth, is a form of love."] },
  },
  cups_14: {
    surface: "The Guidebook",
    keywords: ["Emotional balance", "Mastery of feelings", "Compassion with wisdom", "Diplomacy", "Tolerance", "The wise advisor", "Calm authority"],
    meaning: { label: "What the card means", paras: ["The King of Cups is the water suit in command. Kings are their suit matured into authority, and this one governs the hardest territory: feeling that stays wise under pressure. He sits calm in a moving sea, compassionate without spilling, firm without freezing. Emotional mastery, in this deck, looks exactly this undramatic.", "Drawing him means a situation is asking you to hold feeling and judgment in the same hand: a conflict needing a cool head with a warm read, news that must be delivered kindly and clearly, a role where people bring you their storms. The card's standard is high and reachable. Feel everything, own what you feel, and let none of it commandeer the decision."] },
    reading: { label: "As a reading", paras: ["The King of Cups anchors wherever diplomacy earns its keep. A dispute that needs one adult determined to stay one. A hard conversation that will go as well as its calmest participant. A mentor's moment, yours to give or to seek. His counsel is tolerance with a spine: absorb the heat, honor the feeling, and answer the substance.", "Rule the inner sea by respecting it. Name your feelings early and privately, give the strong ones a hearing before the meeting rather than during, and let compassion set the tone while wisdom sets the terms. People will bring you their worst days for exactly this reason; treat that as the honor it is. Deep feeling, held steady, is what calm authority is actually made of."] },
  },
  swords_01: {
    surface: "The Guidebook",
    keywords: ["A mental breakthrough", "Clarity", "Truth revealed", "Focus", "Decisive action", "Intellectual power", "Cutting through confusion"],
    meaning: { label: "What the card means", paras: ["The Ace of Swords opens the suit of air, the deck's family of mind: thought, truth, words, and the conflicts they start. Every ace is a seed, and this one is an edge: the mental breakthrough, the sudden clean line through a tangled question. One true thought arrives, and the fog concedes.", "Drawing it means clarity is available right now in a way it was not last week. A truth has surfaced, or is about to, and it cuts in the way honest things cut: cleanly, impartially, and without checking whose comfort is at stake. The card calls this a gift and means it. Confusion is expensive, and the mind that suddenly sees pays down every debt at once."] },
    reading: { label: "As a reading", paras: ["The Ace of Swords flashes wherever the fog has been thickest. A decision that untangles the moment it is stated plainly. A hard fact that ends a long negotiation with yourself. The exact words, finally, for something you could not name. Its counsel is to act on the clarity quickly, because sharp edges dull fast in a drawer.", "Use the new edge with care and kindness both. Truth is a scalpel in this suit, never a cudgel, and what it cuts should be confusion rather than people. Say the clear thing in the fewest faithful words, decide what the breakthrough implies, and write it down before the mind files an appeal. Minds cloud again; that is what minds do. A truth written down in daylight survives the next fog."] },
  },
  swords_02: {
    surface: "The Guidebook",
    keywords: ["Indecision", "Stalemate", "A difficult choice", "Avoiding the truth", "Needing a truce", "Blocked vision"],
    meaning: { label: "What the card means", paras: ["The Two of Swords is the air suit's stalemate. It stands for indecision held with great discipline: two options, two crossed blades, and a blindfold worn almost voluntarily. The figure is balanced, composed, and completely stuck, which is the card's honest portrait of how dignified avoidance can look from the outside.", "Drawing it means a choice has been waiting on you long enough to feel permanent. The facts are mostly in, the paths are known, and what not-choosing protects is the comfort of never losing either one. The card is gentle about the cost and clear about it as well. Delay charges rent on ground that looks neutral."] },
    reading: { label: "As a reading", paras: ["The Two of Swords sits very still wherever a decision has stalled. A choice revisited nightly and never made. Two futures kept alive on half-rations. A truth avoided so carefully the avoiding became a schedule. Its counsel is to lift the blindfold first, because most stalemates are protected from information, not starved of it.", "Break the tie with your own values instead of more data. Somewhere beneath the pro-and-con lists, one option already has your loyalty; the ritual of weighing has mostly been a way to avoid saying so. Set a date, choose, and let the unchosen path close respectfully. Losing one future is the toll. Keeping both was never on the menu anyway."] },
  },
  swords_03: {
    surface: "The Guidebook",
    keywords: ["Heartbreak", "Painful realization", "Sorrow", "Loss", "Separation", "A difficult truth", "Clarity through pain"],
    meaning: { label: "What the card means", paras: ["The Three of Swords is the deck's heartbreak, drawn exactly as it feels: three blades, one heart, rain behind. It stands for sorrow, separation, and the kind of painful realization that cannot be argued with. The deck includes it because lives include it, and pretending otherwise would help nobody.", "Drawing it means something pierced: a loss, a parting, a truth that arrived point-first. The card does not minimize, and neither will this page. It hurts, it is real, and it means what you think it means. What the card adds is the sky behind the heart: rain, which is grief doing its work, and clouds, which pass. Clarity through pain is still clarity. It counts, and it stays."] },
    reading: { label: "As a reading", paras: ["The Three of Swords accompanies the losses that reorganize a person. A relationship ending with the truth spoken at last. A separation nobody gets to appeal. Words that keep replaying at their original volume. Its counsel is honesty about the size of it: name what was lost in full, because grief measured honestly can finish, and grief discounted keeps the wound on retainer.", "Do the mourning with company and daylight. Say the plain version of what happened to someone safe, cry at the scheduled and unscheduled times, and refuse the twin tempters, numbness and endless replay. The rain on this card is the mercy in it: heartbreak felt all the way through drains, and heartbreak managed stays full forever. The heart is a muscle before it is a symbol. It heals the way muscles do, through gentle use."] },
  },
  swords_04: {
    surface: "The Guidebook",
    keywords: ["Rest after struggle", "Recuperation", "Mental quiet", "Sanctuary", "Truce", "Contemplation", "Meditation"],
    meaning: { label: "What the card means", paras: ["The Four of Swords is the air suit's sanctuary. It stands for rest after struggle: the mind granted a ceasefire, the armor hung up, the world instructed to wait. Fours in this deck are resting points, and this is the deepest of them, rest as a discipline rather than a collapse.", "Drawing it means the battle is paused, or must be. Something took real effort or real damage, and the next right move is strategic stillness: recovery treated with the same seriousness as the fight. The card is specific about the kind of quiet it means. Sanctuary, here, is protected space chosen on purpose, a room with a door the noise is not allowed through."] },
    reading: { label: "As a reading", paras: ["The Four of Swords watches over needed recoveries. A push that ended and left the tank empty. A conflict that should not be re-entered tired. A mind that has been solving all night and calling it sleep. Its counsel is full permission, because recovery belongs inside the campaign rather than after it.", "Build the sanctuary deliberately. Silence what pings, decline what drains, and give the stillness enough days to actually work; a truce that keeps getting interrupted is just slower war. Let the mind do its quiet consolidation, the kind that happens in naps, walks, and staring usefully out of windows. The swords above the resting figure stay on the wall, within reach all along. Rest, done properly, is how they get sharp again."] },
  },
  swords_05: {
    surface: "The Guidebook",
    keywords: ["Winning at too high a cost", "Conflict", "Loss", "A Pyrrhic victory", "Discord", "Humiliation", "Ambition overriding ethics"],
    meaning: { label: "What the card means", paras: ["The Five of Swords is the air suit's expensive win. It stands for conflict that ended with a victor and no celebration: the argument taken, the room emptied, one figure collecting swords nobody stayed to contest. Fives in this deck are rough patches, and this one is the loneliest, because it looks like triumph right up until the echo.", "Drawing it means a conflict in your orbit is producing winners and losses at the same time, and the accounting deserves a second look. Perhaps you won something lately at a price still being tallied, or you are mid-fight and aiming at a victory of exactly that kind. The card's push is quiet and pointed: it asks what the win is for, and who will be left to enjoy it with you."] },
    reading: { label: "As a reading", paras: ["The Five of Swords lingers after fights that went one point too far. An argument won with a sentence you would like back. A negotiation pressed past fair into scorched. A principle defended until it cost the friendship it was defending. Its counsel is to check the field for what actually fell there, because some victories are defeats wearing the better outfit.", "Choose repair over margin. If the win is already taken, spend some of it on the people it bruised; an apology from the victor costs little and restores almost everything. If the fight is still live, decide what would count as enough and stop there, on purpose, in view of everyone. Ambition needs ethics the way blades need handles. Without the grip, every swing cuts the hand that made it."] },
  },
  swords_06: {
    surface: "The Guidebook",
    keywords: ["Transition", "Passage", "Moving on from difficulty", "A rite of passage", "A melancholy journey", "Finding logical solutions"],
    meaning: { label: "What the card means", paras: ["The Six of Swords is the air suit's crossing. It stands for transition: the decision is made, the worst is behind, and the boat is moving, away from what hurt and toward what will not. Nobody aboard is celebrating, because this is passage rather than victory. The card's mood is honest: relief, melancholy, and forward motion, all in one hull.", "Drawing it means you are mid-transition, past the leaving and short of the arriving. The card endorses the route completely and skips the pep talk: crossings are quiet, gray, and unglamorous, and they are also how people actually get out of hard chapters. Logic is doing the rowing here, and feelings are permitted to ride as passengers. That arrangement, for now, is the correct one."] },
    reading: { label: "As a reading", paras: ["The Six of Swords ferries people out of finished trouble. A move made for sane reasons rather than exciting ones. A recovery that has begun without feeling like one yet. A slow, deliberate exit from a heavy situation. Its counsel is steady oars: keep the practical steps small and repeating, because rhythm carries a crossing better than inspiration does.", "Permit the melancholy without obeying it. Looking back at a receding shore is human; rowing back is the only real mistake. Bring what the hard chapter taught, leave what it charged, and let the far bank be allowed to be better, even if believing that still feels premature. Arrivals rarely announce themselves in this suit. One morning the crossing is simply behind you, and the new ground holds."] },
  },
  swords_07: {
    surface: "The Guidebook",
    keywords: ["Deception", "Strategy", "Acting alone", "Betrayal", "Theft", "Resourcefulness", "Trying to get away with something"],
    meaning: { label: "What the card means", paras: ["The Seven of Swords is the air suit's sneak. It stands for strategy in its gray zone: deception, discretion, the solo move, the plan that works best unannounced. The figure tiptoes off with five swords and leaves two behind, which is the card in one image: clever, mostly successful, and not quite clean.", "Drawing it means somebody in the situation is playing a private game, and the card's first job is helping you consider every candidate, including you. Sometimes this is theft or betrayal in the plain sense. Just as often it is strategy wearing soft shoes: information withheld, a plan advanced quietly, a solo act where the team expected consultation. The card does not sort the ethics for you. It insists only that you see the move."] },
    reading: { label: "As a reading", paras: ["The Seven of Swords slips through wherever candor has gone missing. A story with a version for each audience. A negotiation where someone's real goal stays offstage. A shortcut you are tempted to take while nobody is checking. Its counsel is a quiet inventory, because what gets carried off matters less than what the carrying costs.", "If the sneak is elsewhere, respond with daylight rather than drama: verify, document, and ask the direct question that clever plans hate most. If the sneak is you, do the honest math: strategy is legitimate, discretion is sometimes kind, and a win that must stay hidden from everyone you respect is announcing its price. The two swords left behind are the tell. Nobody takes everything cleanly, and quiet exits keep accounts that eventually get read."] },
  },
  swords_08: {
    surface: "The Guidebook",
    keywords: ["Feeling restricted", "Limitation", "Feeling trapped", "Powerlessness", "Needing new perspective"],
    meaning: { label: "What the card means", paras: ["The Eight of Swords is the air suit's trap, built almost entirely of thought. It stands for feeling restricted: a figure blindfolded and hemmed in by eight planted blades, while the picture quietly includes a gap in the fence, exactly where she is not looking. The trap is real to whoever is inside it. That is the whole problem the card names.", "Drawing it means some limitation in your life is sturdier in the mind than in the world. The powerlessness feels total, and the card honors it as a feeling while disputing it as a fact. Rules never actually checked, permissions never actually asked, an old story about what you cannot do that has not been tested in years: these are the swords in the ground. And swords in the ground are only standing there. Nobody is holding them."] },
    reading: { label: "As a reading", paras: ["The Eight of Swords tightens around lives run by untested assumptions. A job stayed in because leaving is 'impossible.' A conversation avoided because its outcome is 'already known.' A limit inherited so long ago it feels like architecture. Its counsel is to test one of the fence posts this week, gently, and watch what actually happens.", "Give the blindfold its due: perspective is the entire lock. Ask someone outside the fence to describe what they see; borrowed eyes are the fastest exit this card knows, because other people can usually spot the gap in about a minute. Then move, small and real, in the direction of the opening. Freedom here rarely arrives as a rescue. It arrives as a step that turns out, to everyone's quiet astonishment, to have been available all along."] },
  },
  swords_09: {
    surface: "The Guidebook",
    keywords: ["Anxiety", "Worry", "Fear", "Nightmares", "Mental anguish", "Despair", "Negativity"],
    meaning: { label: "What the card means", paras: ["The Nine of Swords is the air suit's bad night. It stands for anxiety, worry, and the mind turned against its owner in the dark: the figure sits up in bed, face in hands, while nine swords hang on the wall behind. Look once more at that wall. The swords are mounted, not falling, and none of them ever touches her.", "Drawing it means worry has been running the night shift: replaying, forecasting, escalating, and presenting its drafts as facts. The card takes the anguish seriously, because a fear does not need to be accurate to be exhausting. But its whole point sits in the gap between the mind's cinema and the room's stillness. This suffering is real, and most of its screenplay is not happening."] },
    reading: { label: "As a reading", paras: ["The Nine of Swords sits up with whoever cannot put the mind down. A dread that grows teeth after midnight. A worry loop that has worn its groove smooth. A fear checked eleven times and never once confirmed. Its counsel is scheduled review: write the fear down and read it again at lunch, when the committee of catastrophes has gone home.", "Treat the anguish as noise in the instrument rather than news from the world, and care for the instrument: sleep, movement, sunlight, and one honest conversation outrank another night of solo analysis. Say the fear out loud to somebody, because spoken worries shrink in company the way they swell in private. And keep the card's own evidence close. The swords stay on the wall, the night ends, and the person in the bed is unharmed."] },
  },
  swords_10: {
    surface: "The Guidebook",
    keywords: ["An absolute ending", "Hitting rock bottom", "Ruin", "Finality", "A painful conclusion", "Betrayal", "Exhaustion after struggle", "Surrender"],
    meaning: { label: "What the card means", paras: ["The Ten of Swords is the deck's most dramatic ending, and it knows it: ten blades in one fallen figure, where two would have done. It stands for the absolute conclusion, the ruin that cannot be argued into anything else. Tens in this deck are completions, and this one completes the hard way: totally, painfully, and, mercifully, for good.", "Drawing it means something has ended beyond revival, or is about to, and the card's strange comfort lives inside that totality. Partial endings string hope along for months; this one lets everybody go home. What failed has fully failed, and that means the exhausting work of keeping it alive is finished as well. The card marks the point in a collapse where certainty finally changes sides."] },
    reading: { label: "As a reading", paras: ["The Ten of Swords lies across the endings that leave no argument. A venture that is over in every way but the paperwork. A betrayal that removed all remaining doubt. A struggle lost so completely that, oddly, the pressure lifts. Its counsel is surrender of the technical kind: stop funding the resurrection, and let the ending be as final as it already is.", "Rest first; meaning later. Total endings flatten a person, and the card grants a full stop before it asks anything else. When you look up, borrow its horizon. The sky in the picture is already lightening behind the blades, because the deck placed its blackest moment at the exact hour dawn gets scheduled. Whatever begins for you next starts unusually clean. A finish this total leaves nothing half-owed, and a person with nothing left to protect is, among other things, free."] },
  },
  swords_11: {
    surface: "The Guidebook",
    keywords: ["A messenger of truth", "Curiosity", "New ideas", "Mental energy", "Truth-seeking", "Vigilance", "Restless energy", "Communication", "Eagerness to learn"],
    meaning: { label: "What the card means", paras: ["The Page of Swords is the air suit's student, and the quickest study in the deck. Pages are students and messengers, and this one gathers information the way the other pages gather feelings or sparks: restlessly, constantly, from every direction at once. Picture a mind on tiptoe, sword up, hair in the wind, delighted by exactly how much there is to figure out.", "Drawing it means your curiosity has caught a scent: a question that will not settle, a subject pulling you into research spirals, a hunch that something in the story deserves a closer look. The card blesses the digging and adds its one requirement, honesty about what gets found. Vigilance without fairness curdles into suspicion, and this Page is a truth-seeker first, a critic second."] },
    reading: { label: "As a reading", paras: ["The Page of Swords darts in wherever questions outnumber answers. A claim that deserves checking before it gets repeated. A new field of knowledge with your name on it. A conversation that needs one brave, clarifying question asked out loud. Its counsel is to stay curious past the first satisfying answer, because the first answer is usually just the loudest.", "Learn in public. Ask the question everyone else is pretending not to have, share what you find before it is polished, and let being corrected count as progress, since to this Page it genuinely is. Keep the vigilance kind: watchfulness aimed at facts sharpens a mind, and aimed at people it merely sharpens the tongue. Fresh sight is a temporary gift. Spend it on the big questions while everything still looks strange."] },
  },
  swords_12: {
    surface: "The Guidebook",
    keywords: ["Decisiveness", "Directness", "Focus", "Ambition", "Charging ahead with ideas", "Haste", "Ruthlessness"],
    meaning: { label: "What the card means", paras: ["The Knight of Swords is the air suit at full charge. Knights take their suit to the extreme, and this one is an argument on horseback: decisive, direct, brilliant in motion, and fully convinced the shortest distance between two points is through. Where the fire Knight chases the thrill, this one chases the conclusion.", "Drawing it means an idea has taken command, in you or near you. A decision wants making now, a case wants stating bluntly, and momentum is treating every hesitation as an insult. The card admires this energy and files one caution with it: speed reads as clarity even when it is only speed. Charge, by all means. Just confirm the destination deserves the horse."] },
    reading: { label: "As a reading", paras: ["The Knight of Swords cuts in wherever momentum and mind have merged. A project that needs one week of ruthless focus. A truth that needs saying plainly, today, in one sitting. A debate you keep winning in your head at full volume. Its counsel is aim before speed: pick the single point that matters and commit the whole charge to it.", "Blunt the ruthlessness, keep the edge. Directness does its best work on problems and its worst on people, so state the case hard and leave the person standing. Build in one deliberate pause before anything irreversible, a breath, a reread, a second opinion, then go without apology. Minds this fast are rare and useful, and the whole art is making sure the speed arrives somewhere worth it."] },
  },
  swords_13: {
    surface: "The Guidebook",
    keywords: ["Independence", "Clear boundaries", "Sharp intellect", "Honesty", "Perception", "Wise through experience"],
    meaning: { label: "What the card means", paras: ["The Queen of Swords is the air suit sharpened into a person. Queens embody their suit as presence, and hers is clarity with a history: independent, perceptive, honest to the bone, and wise in the way only people who have survived something get to be. She says the true thing kindly when possible, and says it regardless.", "Drawing her means the moment wants clear eyes and clean lines: a truth spoken without decoration, a boundary drawn without a preamble, a decision made on evidence rather than hope. The card also honors where this sharpness comes from. Experience, including the painful kind, did the sharpening, and what it produced is a perception few can fool."] },
    reading: { label: "As a reading", paras: ["The Queen of Swords holds the standard wherever truth needs a spine. A conversation that requires the unvarnished version. A boundary that must survive charm, guilt, and repetition. A judgment call that needs a head unclouded by wishing. Her counsel is candor with manners: say it straight, say it once, and let the silence do the repeating.", "Guard the softness behind the steel. Sharp perception can drift into cynicism if it never gets a day off, and the Queen's edge exists to protect things, warmth included, that she has no intention of advertising. Tell the truth and keep the tenderness; the two were never actually rivals. Respect, around her, is simple to earn. Arrive honest, waste no one's time, and never mistake her clarity for coldness twice."] },
  },
  swords_14: {
    surface: "The Guidebook",
    keywords: ["Mental clarity", "Reasoned authority", "Intellectual power", "Ethical judgment", "Clear communication", "The strategist"],
    meaning: { label: "What the card means", paras: ["The King of Swords is the air suit in command. Kings are their suit matured into authority, and this one rules by reason: clear standards, ethical judgment, and communication so precise that arguments simply run out of places to hide. His power is the rarest kind in any deck or any room, authority that can explain itself.", "Drawing him means a situation needs the head of the table occupied by a clear mind: a dispute wanting a fair ruling, a plan wanting rigorous review, a message that must be exact because it will be quoted. The card asks you to hold the standard even when warmth tugs at it, since consistency is what makes fairness believable. Feelings get heard in this court, and the ruling still comes from principles that can be said out loud."] },
    reading: { label: "As a reading", paras: ["The King of Swords convenes wherever judgment must be trusted. A decision that affects people who are not in the room. A conflict needing an arbiter with no favorite. A strategy that deserves cold reading before warm launch. His counsel is to decide on stated criteria, because a judgment that can be explained survives its critics, and one that cannot deserves them.", "Communicate like the ruling matters. Define the terms, show the reasoning, invite the strongest counterargument and answer it in the open; authority spent this way grows, and authority hoarded corrodes. Keep one wise counselor who is unafraid of you, and believe them. The sword points up in his hand for a reason. Truth, held straight, is the one weapon that gets sharper with witnesses."] },
  },
  pents_01: {
    surface: "The Guidebook",
    keywords: ["A seed of potential", "A new opportunity", "Manifestation", "Prosperity", "Grounding", "Tangible results"],
    meaning: { label: "What the card means", paras: ["The Ace of Pentacles opens the suit of earth, the deck's family of the material world: work, money, home, health, and everything ambition becomes once it takes physical form. Every ace is a seed, and this one actually behaves like one. It is a small, concrete opportunity that intends, with tending, to become property, prosperity, or a livelihood.", "Drawing it means a tangible opportunity is in your hand or entering it: an offer, an opening, a first client, a plot of ground in whatever form ground takes for you. The card is the most practical well-wisher in the deck. It promises nothing except that the seed is real and the soil is decent, and it asks for the unglamorous verbs: plant, water, wait, repeat."] },
    reading: { label: "As a reading", paras: ["The Ace of Pentacles takes root where potential gets physical. A job or venture with an actual start date. A first sum saved toward a real thing. A healthy habit finally small enough to keep. Its counsel is groundwork over grand plans: secure the little foundation completely, because everything later stands on how boring you were willing to be now.", "Take the opportunity in both hands and make it concrete within the week: signed, scheduled, planted, opened. Material things grow by compounding, which only ever rewards the early and the regular. Guard the seed stage from both neglect and fuss, and let time do the part only time does. The coin in the card hovers above a garden path for a reason. Wealth of every kind, in this suit, starts as gardening."] },
  },
  pents_02: {
    surface: "The Guidebook",
    keywords: ["Juggling", "Balancing resources", "Adaptability", "Managing priorities", "Ebb and flow", "Change"],
    meaning: { label: "What the card means", paras: ["The Two of Pentacles is the earth suit's balancing act. It stands for adaptability: two live priorities kept in motion by timing rather than muscle. The figure looks light on its feet, and the coins travel a loop shaped like infinity, because this act, the card admits cheerfully, never really ends.", "Drawing it means life is asking you to hold more than one real thing at once: work and home, saving and spending, the steady obligation and the growing dream. The card declines to call this a problem. Managing the ebb and flow is a skill, and you are visibly mid-performance. What it does prescribe is rhythm. Fixed grips drop things; a beat you can repeat keeps them airborne."] },
    reading: { label: "As a reading", paras: ["The Two of Pentacles bobs and weaves through full calendars. Two roles that both deserve real attention this month. A budget doing quiet gymnastics behind a calm exterior. A season where flexibility is outworking every plan. Its counsel is sequence over simultaneity: give each thing its full moment in the hand, then release it cleanly for the next.", "Choose what stays in the air on purpose. Jugglers survive by knowing which items are rubber and which are glass, so sort your commitments honestly and protect the glass without hesitation. Build slack into the loop, because rhythm without margin becomes fragility with good posture. And when the act genuinely exceeds two hands, say so early. Adaptability includes adapting the load."] },
  },
  pents_03: {
    surface: "The Guidebook",
    keywords: ["Teamwork", "Collaboration", "Mastery of a craft", "Apprenticeship", "Initial success through skill", "Recognition for quality work"],
    meaning: { label: "What the card means", paras: ["The Three of Pentacles is the earth suit's job site. It stands for teamwork and craft: the stonemason at work in the cathedral doorway while the architect and the patron lean in, and all three, pointedly, are listening to each other. Skill starts mattering here, and so does the discovery that skill grows fastest in good company.", "Drawing it means the work in front of you wants collaborators, and the card is specific about what makes collaboration real: every voice at the table has a competence the others lack, and every voice gets used. Being consulted is part of it; consulting back is the rest. Quality, in this card, is a group phenomenon, and recognition is starting to arrive because the work has earned it."] },
    reading: { label: "As a reading", paras: ["The Three of Pentacles gets to work wherever skills interlock. A project that needs the specialist, the planner, and the payer at one table. Early professional recognition that deserves to be taken seriously. A craft leaving its amateur phase in real time. Its counsel is to work openly, because craftsmanship reviewed early is craftsmanship improved cheaply.", "Play your position fully and respect the others completely. Say what only you know, ask for what only they know, and let the drawing on the wall settle disagreements; a shared standard beats a strong opinion every time. Take the compliment when the work draws one, and route some of it sideways. Buildings remember their whole crew, even when the plaque names one."] },
  },
  pents_04: {
    surface: "The Guidebook",
    keywords: ["Stability", "Security", "Conservation", "Control of resources", "Possessiveness", "Stinginess", "Fear of loss"],
    meaning: { label: "What the card means", paras: ["The Four of Pentacles is the earth suit holding on. It stands for security and its shadow: one coin underfoot, one hugged to the chest, one balanced on the head, and a figure who cannot wave hello without dropping money. Fours in this deck are resting points, and this one grips the rest a little too hard.", "Drawing it means holding has become the strategy: savings guarded, positions kept, routines defended. The card gives conservation its full due first, since stability is earned and nobody should apologize for a foundation. Then it asks its pointed question about the grip: whether the resources are being held, or the holder is being held. Security that cannot be spent, risked, or shared has quietly changed jobs."] },
    reading: { label: "As a reading", paras: ["The Four of Pentacles clenches around hard-won ground. A budget that survived lean times and still acts like it is in them. A role kept mainly because letting go is unthinkable. A caution that outlived the danger that taught it. Its counsel is an honest look at the grip: keep what protects, loosen what merely reassures.", "Practice small acts of circulation. Spend modestly on something living, give a little where it returns nothing, take one measured risk per season; money and energy are currencies, and currencies only work while moving. Keep the foundation, certainly, and check occasionally that it still has a house on it rather than a vault. Stability, held rightly, funds a life. The life is the point."] },
  },
  pents_05: {
    surface: "The Guidebook",
    keywords: ["Material loss", "Poverty", "Isolation", "Insecurity", "Feeling left out in the cold", "Illness"],
    meaning: { label: "What the card means", paras: ["The Five of Pentacles is the earth suit's winter. It stands for material hardship and the isolation that rides along with it: two figures in the snow, one hurt, one huddled, both outside. Fives in this deck are rough patches, and this one is the coldest. What it never is, anywhere in its meaning, is permanent.", "Drawing it means resources are strained somewhere: money, work, strength, or simply the sense of belonging that scarcity erodes fastest. The card's honesty comes doubled. The cold is real, and so is the lit window the two figures are passing, because the picture places help inside arm's reach and lets the travelers walk by it. Doors do not open to people convinced there are no doors."] },
    reading: { label: "As a reading", paras: ["The Five of Pentacles trudges alongside lean seasons. A financial stretch that is reordering every plan. A setback that arrived with its friend, shame, in tow. An isolation that grew from not wanting to ask again. Its counsel is against the solo version of survival: hard seasons are walkable, and nobody is issued enough warmth to walk them alone.", "Ask, specifically and soon. Name what would actually help to someone who could actually give it, because pride insulates poorly and the people who love you cannot read frost. Accept in the spirit you would give, then pass it forward when the thaw comes, and it does come. The figures in the card keep moving, which is the quiet instruction. Keep moving, keep company, and keep the window in view."] },
  },
  pents_06: {
    surface: "The Guidebook",
    keywords: ["Generosity", "Charity", "Sharing wealth", "Giving and receiving", "Patronage", "Fair distribution", "Paying debts"],
    meaning: { label: "What the card means", paras: ["The Six of Pentacles is the earth suit's generosity. It stands for wealth in motion between people: charity, patronage, fair wages, favors repaid, the whole circulation system of giving and receiving. The merchant in the card holds scales in one hand while giving with the other, because in this card fairness and kindness are the same gesture.", "Drawing it means resources want to move through you in one direction or the other. Perhaps you have surplus and somewhere useful to send it; perhaps you are the one in need this round, which the card treats as an ordinary position in the cycle rather than a lesser one. Its real subject is the exchange itself. Generosity done fairly dignifies both ends, and every giver in this deck has taken a turn receiving."] },
    reading: { label: "As a reading", paras: ["The Six of Pentacles changes hands wherever the flow is being decided. A raise, a rate, or a split that should be set fairly rather than cleverly. A cause that your surplus could quietly transform. An offer of help that pride has been holding at the door. Its counsel is honest scales: give what is real, take what is needed, and keep the exchange clean of strings.", "Give like someone who remembers needing. The best generosity carries no scoreboard and no ceremony; it sizes the gift to the need, hands it over level, and forgets it publicly while remembering it privately. And receive like someone who intends to give later, which is to say gratefully and without groveling. The cycle is the point. Wealth, in this suit, is judged less by the pile than by the current running through it."] },
  },
  pents_07: {
    surface: "The Guidebook",
    keywords: ["Patience", "The long-term view", "Investment", "Waiting for harvest", "Assessing progress", "Perseverance"],
    meaning: { label: "What the card means", paras: ["The Seven of Pentacles is the earth suit leaning on its hoe. It stands for the long-term view at its hardest moment: the crop is planted, the plants are thriving, and the harvest is still months out. This is the card of investments mid-grow, and of the strange labor called waiting, which mostly consists of not digging things up to check.", "Drawing it means something you invested in, money, effort, years, a body of work, has entered its quiet middle, where progress is real and invisible on any given day. The card authorizes the pause you are standing in: assess honestly, adjust if the evidence says so, and otherwise let the roots work. Growth of the durable kind is mostly underground exactly when it looks like nothing."] },
    reading: { label: "As a reading", paras: ["The Seven of Pentacles leans on the fence of long projects. A career investment years from its payoff and on track. A savings plan too young to look impressive. A skill in the plateau that precedes the jump. Its counsel is measured patience: check the fundamentals on a schedule, and between checks, leave the field alone.", "Assess like a farmer rather than a day trader. Ask whether the fundamentals hold, sun, soil, effort, direction, and if they do, let the timeline be the plant's rather than your mood's. Prune what is genuinely failing, without sentiment, and reinvest the attention where growth is honest. Then stand easy. The reward for good planting is mostly still ahead, and impatience, whatever its other talents, has never ripened a thing."] },
  },
  pents_08: {
    surface: "The Guidebook",
    keywords: ["Mastery through repetition", "Craftsmanship", "Diligence", "Attention to detail", "Skill development", "Apprenticeship"],
    meaning: { label: "What the card means", paras: ["The Eight of Pentacles is the earth suit at the workbench. It stands for craft: the same task, repeated with full attention, until the work starts teaching the worker. The apprentice in the card has finished six coins and is bent over the seventh, and that, entirely, is the philosophy.", "Drawing it means the current chapter rewards diligence over brilliance. A skill wants developing the unglamorous way: reps, feedback, corrections, more reps, with attention paid to details nobody else would notice yet. The card is encouraging in its plain manner. This kind of work accumulates quietly, and the distance between amateur and expert is made of exactly these unwitnessed hours."] },
    reading: { label: "As a reading", paras: ["The Eight of Pentacles clocks in wherever skill is under construction. A craft worth practicing past the point of praise. A detail standard that is becoming your signature. An apprenticeship, formal or self-declared, in its long middle. Its counsel is protected repetition: schedule the practice like income depends on it, because eventually it does.", "Love the boring parts; they are load-bearing. Fluency lives in fundamentals, and the practitioners who keep polishing basics long after basics were 'done' are the ones whose work later looks like ease. Seek correction from people whose standards scare you a little, and log the progress, since improvement this gradual hides from memory. Keep the bench tidy and the streak alive. Craft is a long apprenticeship that quietly runs out of teachers."] },
  },
  pents_09: {
    surface: "The Guidebook",
    keywords: ["Self-sufficiency", "Abundance", "Luxury", "Comfort in solitude", "The culmination of hard work", "Independence"],
    meaning: { label: "What the card means", paras: ["The Nine of Pentacles is the earth suit's earned ease. It stands for self-sufficiency: abundance built by one person's discipline and enjoyed, quite happily, in that person's own company. The figure strolls her grounds with a falcon on one glove, both of them trained, both of them calm. Nines sit one step from completion, and this one is enjoying the approach in no hurry at all.", "Drawing it means a stretch of independence has been earned and should be inhabited: comforts paid for by old discipline, a life that runs on your own terms, an afternoon that requires nobody's permission. The card distinguishes this from loneliness without being asked. This is company you chose, your own, in grounds you planted yourself, and the quiet is the trophy rather than the price."] },
    reading: { label: "As a reading", paras: ["The Nine of Pentacles strolls through self-made comfort. A luxury finally bought without wincing, because the work paid for it twice. A home, schedule, or craft shaped precisely to your taste. An independence that took years and fits perfectly. Its counsel is open enjoyment: eat your own harvest, slowly, and in your best clothes.", "Keep the discipline that built the ease; the falcon stays trained even on quiet afternoons. Appetite managed is what made abundance pleasant, and that bargain never expires. Share on your terms, or keep the quiet without explaining; both are dialects of the same fluency. And let the achievement be admitted. Somebody built all this deliberately, over years, against the odds, and she is standing in it."] },
  },
  pents_10: {
    surface: "The Guidebook",
    keywords: ["Legacy", "Inheritance", "Family wealth", "Long-term stability", "Tradition", "Ancestry", "Passing on a foundation"],
    meaning: { label: "What the card means", paras: ["The Ten of Pentacles is the earth suit's long game won. It stands for legacy: family wealth in the broadest sense, homes, names, land, tables, traditions, stable enough to be handed to people not yet born. Tens in this deck are completions, and this one completes past the edge of a single lifetime. Three generations share the card's one courtyard, plus the dogs.", "Drawing it means you are inside something bigger than a personal win: receiving what earlier hands built, or building what later hands will receive, and usually both at once. The card takes the long view literally. Decisions here deserve grading on the horizon of decades, and wealth, at this rank, is measured by how many people it quietly steadies."] },
    reading: { label: "As a reading", paras: ["The Ten of Pentacles sets the multi-generation table. An inheritance arriving in money, property, values, or recipes. A family system, given or chosen, functioning as an actual foundation. A decision about what will outlast you. Its counsel is stewardship: spend from the interest of what you were handed, and add a room before passing it on.", "Tend the people before the portfolio. Legacies transfer through relationships or not at all, so repair the strained ones while everyone is alive to sign off, and tell the stories that make the assets mean something. Write things down, formally and informally; paperwork, done properly, is affection addressed to people you will never meet. The card's real estate is time. Anyone can build for Friday. This card invites you to build for a century."] },
  },
  pents_11: {
    surface: "The Guidebook",
    keywords: ["A messenger of opportunity", "New practical opportunities", "Initial planning", "Learning a craft", "Curiosity about the material world", "A student of a trade"],
    meaning: { label: "What the card means", paras: ["The Page of Pentacles is the earth suit's student, the one who actually does the reading. Pages are students and messengers, and this one studies the tangible: a trade, a market, a craft, a patch of the material world examined coin-first and up close. The other pages chase sparks, feelings, and questions. This one wants to know how things are built.", "Drawing it means a practical ambition is in its studying phase: a skill worth formal learning, a venture worth researching properly, a subject pulling you toward competence rather than mere interest. The card endorses the homework completely, and adds the earth suit's signature requirement. Study must touch ground to count here, so tie every chapter learned to one thing actually attempted."] },
    reading: { label: "As a reading", paras: ["The Page of Pentacles enrolls wherever ambition gets practical. A course, certification, or trade with your future in it. A small venture worth learning by running. A budget, craft, or market being understood for the first time. Its counsel is student posture: ask basic questions proudly, take real notes, and let competence arrive at its own honest pace.", "Keep the dream tied to the desk. Grand plans are cheap and first attempts are precious, so bias toward the small real version: the stall before the store, the sketch before the line, the hundred saved before the thousand planned. Find someone who does it well and study them shamelessly. This Page grows into every other card in the suit, and the growing starts with the page in front of it tonight."] },
  },
  pents_12: {
    surface: "The Guidebook",
    keywords: ["Reliability", "Hard work", "Diligence", "Patience", "Methodical progress", "Responsibility", "Conservatism", "Slow and steady"],
    meaning: { label: "What the card means", paras: ["The Knight of Pentacles is the earth suit's long-haul rider. Knights take their suit to the extreme, and earth taken to its extreme is steadiness: this is the deck's most patient charger, the one whose horse stands still in a plowed field while he decides, thoroughly, where it should step next. The joke lands and the point stands. Slow and steady is also a form of intensity.", "Drawing it means the situation calls for dependability rather than dazzle: methodical progress, promises kept on schedule, the same good work done again today. The card is unglamorous on purpose and unbothered about it. Most things that matter get built by whoever shows up daily after the excitement leaves, and this Knight, famously, has never missed a day."] },
    reading: { label: "As a reading", paras: ["The Knight of Pentacles reports for duty wherever consistency is the strategy. A habit that will change everything in a year and nothing by Friday. A project that needs maintenance more than vision this quarter. A promise worth exactly as much as its keeping. Its counsel is the boring virtues, done proudly: show up, follow through, finish.", "Honor the pace and question it annually. Method is this Knight's genius and his one blind spot, since routines built for progress can quietly become progress's ceiling; keep the discipline, and glance up from the checklist occasionally to confirm the destination has not moved. Let the reliable work be seen without being performed, because trust is this card's real product and it only accrues in public. Then keep going. Ten steady years, in this suit, outbuild any brilliant month."] },
  },
  pents_13: {
    surface: "The Guidebook",
    keywords: ["Nurturing", "Practicality", "Providing", "Down-to-earth", "Sensuality", "Managing home and hearth", "Creating a secure environment"],
    meaning: { label: "What the card means", paras: ["The Queen of Pentacles is the earth suit made welcoming. Queens embody their suit as presence, and hers is practical care: the well-run home, the meal that appears on time, the security other people get to take for granted because she never does. Of all the deck's royalty, she is the one most likely to fix the problem while everyone else is still describing it.", "Drawing her means the moment wants care with its sleeves rolled up: providing, organizing, feeding, funding, the concrete verbs of love. The card holds sensuality and spreadsheets in the same hand and sees no contradiction, since a soft landing for everyone is, behind the scenes, a logistics operation. Somebody makes the secure environment happen. This card is for that somebody, and for noticing when you are it."] },
    reading: { label: "As a reading", paras: ["The Queen of Pentacles runs the household of whatever needs running. A home, team, or budget that thrives on her kind of attention. A person who needs practical help more than a pep talk. A body asking for real food and real sleep. Her counsel is grounded generosity: feed things, literally where possible, and watch most problems shrink to their true size.", "Provide sustainably. The table she sets includes her own chair, so put rest, pleasure, and small earthy delights on your own menu with the same reliability you offer everyone else. Accept help in the kitchen of your life; sharing the providing is a form of providing. And take the compliment the card is paying. Making people materially safe and genuinely comfortable is not a lesser art. Whole childhoods are remembered by its details."] },
  },
  pents_14: {
    surface: "The Guidebook",
    keywords: ["Material success", "Mastery of the material world", "Grounded authority", "Stability", "Abundance", "Enterprise", "Security", "The provider", "Dependability"],
    meaning: { label: "What the card means", paras: ["The King of Pentacles is the earth suit in command, and the deck's final court card: the suit of work and wealth matured into a provider whose enterprise actually holds. He stands for material mastery, stability, and the settled kind of success that no longer needs to prove anything by lunchtime. He built the chair he sits in, and he read every contract on the way to it.", "Drawing him means the moment rewards the owner's mindset: long-term stewardship, generous provision, decisions made like someone who plans to still be here in twenty years. Abundance at this rank is a system to maintain rather than a score to run up: enterprises steadied, people provided for, resources placed where they quietly keep everything else standing. The card's confidence is earned and transferable. Build the way he builds, and things stay built."] },
    reading: { label: "As a reading", paras: ["The King of Pentacles takes the head chair wherever material judgment is needed. A financial decision that wants experience instead of optimism. A venture ready for grown-up structure and patient capital. A family or team that needs its provider steady this season. His counsel is dependability at scale: be the person whose yes funds things and whose word closes them.", "Underwrite more than you command. Use the security you have built to make other people's good work possible: back the apprentice, fund the sensible risk, keep the standing offer of a meal and a job reference open. Guard against the throne's one trap, which is mistaking the wealth for the point; gold is this King's tool, and the table and the people at it were always the purpose. End where the suit ends. Provide, steadily, and enjoy what providing built."] },
  },
};

